www.mdgamblinghelp.org (MD), 1-800-981-0023 (PR). 21+ and present in most states. (18+ DC/KY/NH/PR/WY). Void in ONT. Eligibility restrictions apply. Terms: draftkings.com/sportsbook. On behalf of Boot Hill Casino (KS). Pass-thru of per wager tax may apply in IL.\nCopyright: © 2026 ESPN Enterprises, LLC. All rights reserved.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/joe-burrows-start-to-2026-puts-him-in-mvp-contention-bengals-in-afc-title-race/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/joe-burrows-start-to-2026-puts-him-in-mvp-contention-bengals-in-afc-title-race/","url_hash":"63ea9a891f520cb95438b8a02a354cbc6ce4e33711210b6f3d0b21be9a6aefec","content_hash":"9e025a50d7f227e1d3f7eecaff1dc5b10a7500a26bcd15f263185df765f56f46","title":"Joe Burrow&#8217;s start to 2026 puts him in MVP contention, Bengals in AFC title race","summary":"Joe Burrow has opened the 2026 NFL season with a career-best 91.2 PFF grade through seven weeks, ranking second among qualifying quarterbacks. The Cincinnati Bengals quarterback leads the NFL with 1.54 Wins Above Replacement and delivered a 95.0 passing grade in the second half of Cincinnati's comeback win over Baltimore. With a history of elite late-season performances and the league's easiest remaining schedule, Burrow and the Bengals have a path toward AFC contention.","published_at":"2026-10-29T10:30:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Career-best start: Joe Burrow has earned a 91.2 PFF grade through seven weeks of 2026, his highest mark at this stage of any season since entering the NFL.\nStrong second-half track record: Since 2020, Burrow has earned a 95.5 PFF passing grade from Week 8 through the playoffs, the highest among qualifying quarterbacks, as Cincinnati prepares for the NFL's easiest remaining schedule.\nWins above replacement (WAR): Burrow leads all NFL quarterbacks with 1.54 WAR through seven weeks. His lead is narrow, with Dak Prescott second at 1.52 WAR, followed by C.J. Stroud (1.37), Brock Purdy (1.28) and Bryce Young (1.24).\nAt halftime of the Cincinnati Bengals' AFC North game against the Baltimore Ravens on Sunday, the Bengals found themselves in a 13-0 hole. Little had gone right for their offense in the first half, and they were staring down the barrel of a divisional loss against a backup quarterback coming out of their bye week. Even on the road, that would have been a bad result for the Bengals .\nAnd then Joe Burrow locked in.\nThe former first overall pick earned a 95.0 PFF passing grade in the second half, going 16-for-18 for 249 yards and four touchdowns. It was the best half of football we've seen from any quarterback in the NFL this season, with Burrow dictating the game to the Ravens ' defense like he was conducting an orchestra.\nBut it's not just about the second half of that game. What we've seen from Burrow through the first seven weeks of the season has been the best start of his career. Burrow has earned a 91.2 PFF grade, which ranks second in the NFL among quarterbacks with at least 100 dropbacks.\nAnd here's how that compares to every other season of his career.\nJoe Burrow: PFF grade from Week 1-7\nSeason\nPFF grade\n2020\n74.6\n2021\n86.4\n2022\n77.0\n2023\n66.4\n2024\n85.7\n2025\n78.8\n2026\n91.2\nThrough seven weeks, Burrow has completed 157 of his 219 passes for 1,653 yards, 15 touchdowns and just three interceptions, earning a 110.4 passer rating in the process. His 19.9% positively graded play rate ranks third among qualifying quarterbacks, but his negatively graded play rate of just 5.4% is the lowest.\nHe's recorded 12 big-time throws, tied for the fourth-most in the NFL. His 5.3% big-time throw rate ranks fifth, and his 1.6% turnover-worthy play rate ranks sixth-lowest. His 78.7% adjusted completion percentage and 0.21 EPA per play both rank seventh.\nJackson\nBurrow\nPurdy\nPrescott\nYoung\nGoff\nAllen\nStroud\nMaye\nDaniels\nMahomes\nBrissett\nSmith\nCousins\nWilliams\nShough\nLove\nMurray\nLawrence\nWard\nDarnold\nPenix Jr.\nStafford\nMcKee\nMariota\nRodgers\nHuntley\nWentz\nHurts\nMayfield\nWatson\nBagent\nWinston\nJones\nWillis\nNix\nDaniels\nHerbert The same players and metrics are available as a sortable table on each team's Leaders tab.\nSource: Premium Stats Pro\nLamar Jackson\nQB · BLT\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 20.6% 2nd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.3% 4th / 38\nJoe Burrow\nQB · CIN\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 19.9% 3rd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 5.4% 1st / 38\nBrock Purdy\nQB · SF\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 19.2% 6th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.0% 3rd / 38\nDak Prescott\nQB · DAL\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 19.3% 5th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.9% 25th / 38\nBryce Young\nQB · CAR\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 16.6% 11th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.8% 23rd / 38\nJared Goff\nQB · DET\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 16.9% 9th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.6% 20th / 38\nJosh Allen\nQB · BUF\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 21.2% 1st / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 7.5% 12th / 38\nC.J. Stroud\nQB · HST\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 16.2% 13th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 7.4% 9th / 38\nDrake Maye\nQB · NE\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 19.4% 4th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.7% 22nd / 38\nJayden Daniels\nQB · WAS\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 18.5% 7th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.3% 4th / 38\nPatrick Mahomes\nQB · KC\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.3% 25th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.9% 7th / 38\nJacoby Brissett\nQB · ARZ\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 16.9% 9th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 7.4% 9th / 38\nGeno Smith\nQB · NYJ\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 11.9% 34th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 5.8% 2nd / 38\nKirk Cousins\nQB · LV\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.9% 16th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.1% 14th / 38\nCaleb Williams\nQB · CHI\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.7% 17th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.3% 4th / 38\nTyler Shough\nQB · NO\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 15.3% 14th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 9.6% 29th / 38\nJordan Love\nQB · GB\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 16.3% 12th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 11.4% 36th / 38\nKyler Murray\nQB · MIN\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.3% 25th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.3% 17th / 38\nTrevor Lawrence\nQB · JAX\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 17.2% 8th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.6% 20th / 38\nCam Ward\nQB · TEN\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.3% 20th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 7.5% 12th / 38\nSam Darnold\nQB · SEA\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.8% 22nd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 6.9% 7th / 38\nMichael Penix Jr.\nQB · ATL\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 11.6% 35th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.4% 18th / 38\nMatthew Stafford\nQB · LA\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.2% 21st / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.1% 31st / 38\nTanner McKee\nQB · PHI\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.1% 28th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 9.2% 26th / 38\nMarcus Mariota\nQB · WAS\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.5% 23rd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.1% 14th / 38\nAaron Rodgers\nQB · PIT\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 11.5% 36th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.5% 32nd / 38\nTyler Huntley\nQB · BLT\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.4% 24th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 7.4% 9th / 38\nCarson Wentz\nQB · MIN\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.2% 27th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.5% 19th / 38\nJalen Hurts\nQB · PHI\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.7% 17th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.0% 30th / 38\nBaker Mayfield\nQB · TB\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 13.1% 28th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 9.4% 28th / 38\nDeshaun Watson\nQB · CLV\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 15.3% 14th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.6% 33rd / 38\nTyson Bagent\nQB · CHI\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.1% 38th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.1% 14th / 38\nJameis Winston\nQB · NYG\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.6% 30th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.9% 38th / 38\nDaniel Jones\nQB · IND\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.5% 31st / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.6% 33rd / 38\nMalik Willis\nQB · MIA\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 14.6% 19th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.0% 37th / 38\nBo Nix\nQB · DEN\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 10.7% 37th / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 8.8% 23rd / 38\nJalon Daniels\nQB · TB\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.4% 32nd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 11.0% 35th / 38\nJustin Herbert\nQB · LAC\nPositively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 12.1% 33rd / 38\nNegatively Graded Play Rate · Graded Plays 9.3% 27th / 38\nBut why does Burrow's hot start matter?\nWell, since Burrow entered the NFL in 2020, he has earned a 95.5 PFF grade from Week 8 through the end of the season, including the playoffs. Among the 28 quarterbacks with at least 1,000 qualifying dropbacks over that span, that is comfortably the best mark in the NFL.\nPFF Passing Grade: Week 8 through the Super Bowl (2020-25)\nRank\nQB\nTeam\nPFF Grade\n1\nJoe Burrow\nCincinnati Bengals\n95.5\n2\nJosh Allen\nBuffalo Bills\n91.6\n3\nMatthew Stafford\nLos Angeles Rams\n91.4\n4\nJustin Herbert\nLos Angeles Chargers\n90.5\n5\nPatrick Mahomes\nKansas City Chiefs\n90.5\nminimum 1,000 dropbacks\nSo we have Joe Burrow , who has been the best quarterback in football over the final 10 weeks of the regular season and the playoffs since he entered the NFL, and he just happens to be enjoying the best start to a season of his career.\nIt stands to reason that if he can continue his usual second-half form, he will probably finish as the highest-graded quarterback in the NFL this season.\nThat should be particularly good news for a Bengals team that faces the easiest remaining strength of schedule in the NFL this season. They have arguably the best quarterback in football right now, entering the stretch of the season in which he has historically been at his most dominant, with the league's easiest schedule ahead of him.\nWith all of that working in their favor, there's little reason to believe the Bengals can't be serious contenders for the No. 1 seed in the AFC by the end of the regular season. Their Week 14 matchup against the Kansas City Chiefs could prove pivotal in that race.\nIf the Bengals and Burrow continue playing at this level, both the team and its quarterback could be in line to take home some serious hardware this season.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-special-teams-report-nfl-week-7/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-special-teams-report-nfl-week-7/","url_hash":"eba631f450c64303c6c3c8a2ab102db223b16ea69adbce5f5a4669655656de39","content_hash":"3473f93af13938a07771a1343ed2b50321ede237c97dffa5cfb9a67553bd258d","title":"NFL Special Teams Report: NFL Week 7","summary":"PFF's NFL Week 7 Special Teams Report highlights standout performances from gunners, vise players, long snappers and kick coverage and return units. Indianapolis Colts defensive tackle Grover Stewart earns the play of the week for his blocked field goal against Minnesota, while teammates Ashton Dulin and Cam Taylor-Britt lead the way among gunners.","published_at":"2026-10-29T10:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Grover Stewart delivers the play of the week: The Indianapolis Colts defensive tackle blocked a field goal against the Minnesota Vikings , using his quickness off the snap to get into position.\nColts gunners stand out: Ashton Dulin and Cam Taylor-Britt were the only two NFL players to record multiple gunner rep wins in Week 7, with Dulin turning both wins into tackles.\nTitans lead kick coverage grades: Julius Chestnut (86.3) and Truman Jones (84.0) earned two of the three highest PFF grades among qualifying kick coverage players, while Seattle's Brady Russell posted an 83.1.\nEach week, I'll bring you a report covering everything you need to know about special teams players around the NFL. But this isn't your typical breakdown of kickers, punters and returners. We'll dig deeper into the players who often fly under the radar, including gunners, vise players, long snappers and members of kick return and coverage units. I'll also highlight my favorite special teams plays of the week.\nHere's the Special Teams Report for Week 7.\nSpecial teams play of the week\nGrover Stewart blocked a field goal for the Colts in their loss to the Minnesota Vikings , and there was a lot to like about this play. First, he's incredibly quick off the ball. He then immediately jumps to the other side of the long snapper, putting himself in position to get an arm in the way of what was a low-trajectory kick because of the distance.\nThis was cool by Grover Stewart.\n1. Excellent get off. Think he's the first Colts player to move after the snap.\n2. Immediately flips over to the other side of the LS.\n3. Arm right in the kicking path pic.twitter.com/Bkx1F6Ju2t\n- Gordon McGuinness (@GordonNFL) October 28, 2026\nGunners\nJust two players in the NFL recorded multiple rep wins as gunners in Week 7, and both play for the Indianapolis Colts . Ashton Dulin turned his two wins into a pair of tackles, while teammate Cam Taylor-Britt recorded an assist and helped keep the ball out of the end zone on a punt that was downed at the 5-yard line.\nVise/jammers\nOn the other side of the matchup are the vise players and jammers tasked with slowing down opposing gunners. Five players who logged at least three snaps in that role recorded a vise win percentage of 33.3% or better in Week 7.\nPlayer Team Vise win percentage\nMalachi Moore New York Jets 33.3%\nPJ Jules Cincinnati Bengals 33.3%\nNehemiah Pritchett Seattle Seahawks 33.3%\nAmeer Speed Dallas Cowboys 33.3%\nTycen Anderson Denver Broncos 33.3%\nLong snappers\nLong snappers earn high PFF grades by limiting inaccurate snaps and making tackles in punt coverage. Two long snappers earned PFF grades above 75.0 in Week 7.\nPlayer Team PFF grade\nBen Mann New York Giants 81.5\nNick Moore Baltimore Ravens 78.4\nBoth players recorded a tackle without missing any in punt coverage. However, Moore also had one inaccurate snap, while Mann did not.\nKick coverage units\nPlayers on kick coverage units earn high PFF grades not only by making tackles but also by defeating blocks and influencing kick returners. Among players who logged at least five kick coverage snaps in Week 7, three earned PFF grades above 80.0.\nPlayer Team PFF grade\nJulius Chestnut Tennessee Titans 86.3\nTruman Jones Tennessee Titans 84.0\nBrady Russell Seattle Seahawks 83.1\nKick return units\nPlayers on kick return units earn positive and negative grades based on how effectively they block opposing coverage players. In Week 7, just two players who logged at least five snaps on kick return units earned PFF grades above 75.0.\nPlayer Team PFF grade\nShaun Dolac Los Angeles Rams 76.2\nTim Patrick New York Jets 76.2","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-rookie-rankings/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-rookie-rankings/","url_hash":"cab9efbd781b72a459445518a2d2d0bd3420e67553a087e7ca2e6649b49643eb","content_hash":"fd19e8f3f71c6de087c58ad28fd5fa8ad465701be088d4141b882cc078c6c7a9","title":"NFL rookie rankings: Highest-graded players after Week 7","summary":"Now seven weeks into the NFL season, we&#8217;re starting to get a clearer picture of how this year&#8217;s rookies are performing, with several first-round picks beginning to rise to the top of the class. As we highlight the 15 highest-graded rookies in the NFL, the key caveat is that players must average at least 20 &#8230; Continue reading \"NFL rookie rankings: Highest-graded players after Week 7\"","published_at":"2026-10-29T10:00:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Emmanuel McNeil-Warren leads all rookies: The Cleveland Browns safety ranks No. 1 with an 89.8 PFF overall grade, followed closely by Carolina Panthers safety Zakee Wheatley at 89.3.\nBrowns place three players in the top 15: McNeil-Warren, offensive tackle Spencer Fano (76.9) and wide receiver KC Concepcion (71.9) all make the list, with Fano earning a league-leading 92.4 PFF run-blocking grade in Week 7.\nGabe Jacas leads the rookie pass-rushing group: The New England Patriots edge defender ranks third overall with an 81.1 PFF grade and second among rookies with 21 pressures.\nNow seven weeks into the NFL season, we're starting to get a clearer picture of how this year's rookies are performing, with several first-round picks beginning to rise to the top of the class.\nAs we highlight the 15 highest-graded rookies in the NFL, the key caveat is that players must average at least 20 snaps per game to qualify. That means a player must have logged at least 140 snaps through the first seven weeks to make this list.\nHere are our highest-graded NFL rookies through Week 7.\n1. S Emmanuel McNeil-Warren , Cleveland Browns (89.8)\nThe highest-graded rookie in the NFL this season, McNeil-Warren moved back into a reserve role with fellow safety Grant Delpit returning from injury in Week 7. He has earned PFF grades of 82.3 against the run and 82.2 in coverage while missing just 8.0% of his tackle attempts.\n2. S Zakee Wheatley , Carolina Panthers (89.3)\nWheatley has become a key part of the Panthers ' defense as a rookie, logging 211 snaps through five games. Six of his 19 tackles this season have resulted in defensive stops, and he has recorded a pair of pass breakups. However, he has also missed a tackle in each of the past three games.\n3. EDGE Gabe Jacas , New England Patriots (81.1)\nA second-round draft pick in April, Jacas continues to go from strength to strength as a rookie in New England. He recorded a career-high five pressures in Thursday Night Football's loss to the Chicago Bears and now ranks second among rookies with 21 pressures this season.\n4. CB Mansoor Delane , Kansas City Chiefs (79.7)\nDelane leads all rookie cornerbacks with a 77.4 PFF coverage grade, though he is coming off the lowest-graded game of his career. In a tough matchup against the Seattle Seahawks , he allowed a career-high 90 receiving yards in coverage and surrendered his first touchdown of the season.\n5. LB Kendal Daniels , Atlanta Falcons (77.7)\nDaniels was phenomenal for the Atlanta Falcons in Sunday's win over the San Francisco 49ers , earning a 90.5 PFF grade. He recorded 10 solo tackles, three of which resulted in defensive stops, and came away with his first career interception.\n6. LB Arvell Reese , New York Giants (77.0)\nReese continues to be a fantastic all-around player for the Giants in his rookie season. He has earned PFF grades above 72.0 both as a run defender and in coverage, while his 23 defensive stops rank second among all rookies.\n7. S Robert Spears-Jennings , Pittsburgh Steelers (77.0)\nSpears-Jennings delivered another solid performance for the Steelers in Sunday's win over the Saints in Paris, earning a 76.5 PFF grade. He has yet to earn a PFF grade below 60.0 in a game during his young career and has missed just one of his 15 tackle attempts as a rookie.\n8. OT Spencer Fano , Cleveland Browns (76.9)\nFano has already impressed in pass protection , allowing just five pressures over the Browns ' past four games, but he was at his best as a run blocker against the Tennessee Titans . He earned a 92.4 PFF run-blocking grade, a mark that led all offensive linemen in the NFL in Week 7.\n9. EDGE Akheem Mesidor , Los Angeles Chargers (75.8)\nThe Chargers were on their bye in Week 7, but Mesidor has established himself as a well-rounded player in his rookie season, earning a 75.8 PFF grade. He has registered 10 pressures, including seven combined sacks and quarterback hits, while adding nine tackles resulting in defensive stops, excluding sacks.\n10. OT Caleb Lomu , New England Patriots (75.1)\nLomu didn't play in Week 7, but he has impressed in limited action as a rookie. Across 81 pass-blocking snaps, he has allowed just three pressures, earning an 80.2 PFF pass-blocking grade. Given his performance so far, it may be difficult to keep him out of the lineup as the season progresses.\n11. LB Harold Perkins, Atlanta Falcons (74.3)\nPerkins is the second Falcons rookie linebacker to crack the top 15, and his role has expanded into more of a three-down role in recent weeks. After logging just 15 snaps against the run over his first five games, he has played 34 run-defense snaps over the past two weeks. However, PFF run-defense grades of 56.0 and 44.4 in those games have pulled his overall grade down.\n12. WR Antonio Williams , Washington Commanders (74.0)\nWilliams and the Commanders were on their bye in Week 7, but he impressed in Week 6, catching all three of his targets for the second straight game and turning them into 50 yards in a Monday Night Football loss to the San Francisco 49ers . He has averaged 1.51 yards per route run as a rookie and could take on a bigger role in the offense following Terry McLaurin 's injury .\n13. TE Eli Raridon , New England Patriots (72.5)\nRaridon was once again a bigger factor in the passing game in the Patriots ' Thursday Night Football loss to the Chicago Bears , catching three of four targets for 56 yards and a touchdown. After seeing just three targets over his first three NFL games, he has drawn 12 over the past three, with his role continuing to grow as he approaches the halfway point of his rookie season.\n14. WR Caleb Douglas , Miami Dolphins (72.1)\nDouglas returned to the field for the first time since Week 2 in the Dolphins ' win over the New York Jets , turning three targets into two receptions for 70 yards, with both catches going for touchdowns. His sample size is smaller than that of several other rookies, but he has posted an impressive 2.41 yards per route run across his first three NFL games.\n15. WR KC Concepcion , Cleveland Browns (71.9)\nConcepcion finally delivered the breakout performance he had been threatening since the start of his rookie season, drawing 10 targets in the Browns ' win over the Tennessee Titans on Sunday. He turned those opportunities into eight receptions for 129 yards, earning a career-high 87.6 PFF grade in the process.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-running-game-matchups-to-target/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-running-game-matchups-to-target/","url_hash":"33122b07d1bf9b24bf3a78df34ac734c68f5e0d0ce661f16224b3793a4111f13","content_hash":"c479e6613926bf9d346b346e717a01e8279cd8dadae119b39cdc6c91efe58d58","title":"Fantasy Football Week 8: Running game matchups to target","summary":"Bengals running backs could produce high-level fantasy football results given their Week 8 matchup against the Titans.","published_at":"2026-10-29T09:30:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nic Bodiford","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Jacksonville Jaguars vs. Indianapolis Colts : Jacksonville should run the ball voluminously in an expected blowout.\nCincinnati Bengals vs. Tennessee Titans : No team has been run on more than the Titans.\nLos Angeles Chargers vs. Los Angeles Rams : Both backfields could play productively.\nGames featuring two high-powered offenses and/or defensive disadvantages can create favorable fantasy football matchups that increase a given player’s potential fantasy-scoring output. Identifying these ahead of time gives fantasy managers an edge over the competition. The article below breaks down three running game matchups to target in Week 8.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-passing-game-matchups-to-target/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-passing-game-matchups-to-target/","url_hash":"0c33ef9b46a1ddcd37c4513a7304aab377396782f8c02b81518c518b6a4281e4","content_hash":"4fcee790dc378173e92d96fb240f3b2b28bf6accd5e2dca035ed99951ff56ce2","title":"Fantasy Football Week 8: Passing game matchups to target","summary":"The Cardinals' and Cowboys' passing offenses are two that could yield high-end results for fantasy football managers in Week 8.","published_at":"2026-10-29T09:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nic Bodiford","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Arizona Cardinals vs. Dallas Cowboys : This is Week 8’s highest-total game.\nCarolina Panthers vs. Green Bay Packers : Carolina and Green Bay both rank in the top four among NFL offenses in explosive pass plays.\nLas Vegas Raiders vs. New York Jets : Both teams run pass-first offenses.\nGames featuring two high-powered offenses and/or defensive disadvantages can create favorable fantasy football matchups that increase a given player’s potential fantasy-scoring output. Identifying these ahead of time gives fantasy managers an edge over the competition. The article below breaks down three passing game matchups to target in Week 8.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-start-em-sit-em/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-start-em-sit-em/","url_hash":"ee6d2fae01edcf1eb88887af572dae52f51146bf047c20a0a6355f1649dc0fa4","content_hash":"02ecc722bc376457643e7694b3b31cc65630b8ae8a5c3e14109a10051d8cae92","title":"Fantasy Football Week 8: Start ’em, sit ’em","summary":"Rams running back Blake Corum is a player to elevate in Week 8, while Colts receiver Keenan Allen could see an elevated role given injuries and game script.","published_at":"2026-10-29T09:00:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nathan Jahnke","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-wide-receiver-rankings/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-wide-receiver-rankings/","url_hash":"9fb46b36a234e9b637ca7e07b0576020c8d0dd9ff246121e4d331fb4cb4982a4","content_hash":"146efb104915ad6b7b98b0781b46ad72d1e172c05d1de0ce6dd4b8fb5debfe63","title":"NFL wide receiver rankings: Jaxon Smith-Njigba leads after Week 7, Zay Flowers rises to No. 2","summary":"PFF's NFL wide receiver rankings entering Week 8 of the 2026 season feature Jaxon Smith-Njigba at No. 1, followed by Zay Flowers and Tee Higgins. Higgins climbs nine spots after a strong performance against the Ravens, while Malik Nabers moves into the top 10 following four consecutive 100-yard games. Explore the top 32 receivers through seven weeks, with PFF receiving grades, yards per route run and weekly ranking changes.","published_at":"2026-10-28T13:45:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Jaxon Smith-Njigba holds No. 1: The Seattle Seahawks receiver leads the NFL in receiving yards by 216, maintaining the top spot in PFF's wide receiver rankings entering Week 8.\nTee Higgins makes a major jump: The Cincinnati Bengals receiver climbs nine spots to No. 3 after a standout performance against Baltimore, with his 90.9 PFF receiving grade tied for third in the NFL.\nMalik Nabers continues his rise: The New York Giants receiver moves up six spots to No. 8 after recording four consecutive 100-yard receiving games.\nWelcome to the post-Week 7 edition of our wide receiver rankings for the 2026 season.\nPreseason priors carried significant weight in the early rankings, but seven weeks of football have given us a much stronger foundation for our analysis. At this point, the rankings are driven primarily by what receivers have shown through their usage and performance in 2026, while players with long-term injuries have been removed from consideration.\nWith that in mind, here are our updated wide receiver rankings heading into Week 8.\n1. Jaxon Smith-Njigba , Seattle Seahawks (no change)\nIt's not just that Smith-Njigba leads the NFL in receiving yards. It's that he leads the league by a staggering 216 yards. The gap between him and second-place CeeDee Lamb is the same as the gap between Lamb and George Kittle , who ranks 20th.\n2. Zay Flowers , Baltimore Ravens (up 3)\nFlowers has missed the equivalent of two games this season, while his starting quarterback has missed three and a half. Despite those absences, the Ravens receiver is on pace to break the franchise's single-season receiving yards record by 260 yards.\n3. Tee Higgins , Cincinnati Bengals (up 9)\nHiggins was already enjoying a great season, but he took his play to another level with his best performance of the year in Sunday's comeback win over the Baltimore Ravens . His 90.9 PFF receiving grade through seven weeks is tied for third in the NFL.\n4. CeeDee Lamb , Dallas Cowboys (no change)\nLamb ranks second in the NFL in receiving yards through seven weeks and seventh in average yards per route run at 2.67. He is also tied for fifth among wide receivers with eight missed tackles forced and ranks seventh with an 89.3 PFF receiving grade.\n5. Mike Evans , San Francisco 49ers (down 3)\nEvans slips a few spots this week thanks to standout performances from other receivers around the league, but he emerges from the 49ers ' bye week with the second-highest PFF receiving grade at the position. He is also one of just four wide receivers averaging at least 3.00 yards per route run.\n6. Puka Nacua , Los Angeles Rams (up 2)\nSince returning from injury four weeks ago, Nacua has racked up 411 receiving yards, trailing only Malik Nabers of the New York Giants and Jaxon Smith-Njigba over that span. His 2.68 yards per route run ranks sixth at the position this season.\n7. Drake London , Atlanta Falcons (down 4)\nLondon has been held below 70 receiving yards in each of the past two weeks, but he remains one of just four wide receivers averaging more than 3.00 yards per route run this season. His 90.0 PFF receiving grade ranks sixth at the position.\n8. Malik Nabers , New York Giants (up 6)\nDespite the changes at quarterback for the Giants , Nabers has put together four consecutive 100-yard receiving games, leading the NFL in receiving yards over that stretch. He ranks 12th in PFF receiving grade and ninth in yards per route run for the season, but his recent form is as good as anyone's in the league.\n9. Christian Watson , Green Bay Packers (down 3)\nWatson was held under 60 receiving yards for the third time this season in Sunday's loss to the Detroit Lions , but he still ranks seventh among wide receivers with 595 yards and is tied for second with six receiving touchdowns.\n10. Chris Olave , New Orleans Saints (down 1)\nOlave has cooled off over the past three weeks, totaling just 145 receiving yards after racking up 491 over his first four games. Despite that recent slump, he still ranks third among wide receivers with 636 receiving yards and is tied for eighth with an 87.8 PFF receiving grade.\n11. Ja’Marr Chase , Cincinnati Bengals (up 2)\nIt's been a different kind of season for Chase, with teammate Tee Higgins putting up bigger numbers. That doesn't mean Chase isn't playing well, though. Even in a game where the Baltimore Ravens made him a focal point of their defensive game plan, Chase managed to find the end zone on one of the rare occasions he drew a one-on-one matchup against cornerback Nate Wiggins .\n12. Justin Jefferson , Minnesota Vikings (down 1)\nJefferson has yet to eclipse 100 receiving yards in a game this season and is averaging just six targets per contest, but he's still making an impact despite the limited opportunities. His 86.1 PFF receiving grade ranks 10th among wide receivers, while his 2.44 yards per route run ranks 11th.\n13. Amon-Ra St. Brown , Detroit Lions (up 5)\nAfter three relatively quiet games, St. Brown emerged from the Lions ' bye week with his best performance of the season, hauling in nine of 11 targets for 153 yards. His 2.34 yards per route run through seven weeks ranks 14th among wide receivers.\n14. Tetairoa McMillan , Carolina Panthers (down 7)\nMcMillan managed just 40 receiving yards against the Tampa Bay Buccaneers on Sunday, his second-lowest total of the season. Still, it's been an impressive start to his second NFL campaign, with McMillan ranking inside the top 16 among wide receivers in both PFF receiving grade and yards per route run.\n15. Luther Burden III , Chicago Bears (up 6)\nOne of this week's biggest risers, Burden delivered a standout performance in the Bears ' Thursday Night Football win over the New England Patriots . He set season highs in both receiving yards and PFF receiving grade and now ranks eighth among wide receivers in yards per route run at 2.56.\n16. Matthew Golden , Green Bay Packers (down 6)\nGolden is one of this week's biggest fallers, ironically coming off just his second game of the season without a dropped pass. He ranks ninth among wide receivers with 540 receiving yards, but his seven drops are two more than any other wide receiver in the NFL.\n17. Parker Washington , Jacksonville Jaguars (no change)\nWashington had a pair of drops in the Jaguars ' final game before their bye week. He returns to action this week ranked 13th among wide receivers with an 82.6 PFF receiving grade and 12th with an average of 2.39 yards per route run.\n18. Nico Collins , Houston Texans (down 2)\nCollins was held to just two receptions for six yards on five targets across 30 receiving snaps in the Texans ' overtime loss to the Giants . Despite the quiet outing, he has been one of the NFL's best in contested-catch situations this season, tying for the league lead with 13 contested catches while posting an impressive 81.3% contested-catch rate.\n19. George Pickens , Dallas Cowboys (up 3)\nPickens is tied with Collins for the league lead with 13 contested catches, although his 59.1% contested-catch rate is considerably lower. He ranks outside the top 20 among wide receivers in both PFF receiving grade and yards per route run, but his 485 receiving yards are tied for 13th at the position.\n20. DeVonta Smith , Philadelphia Eagles (down 5)\nSmith's 90.2 PFF receiving grade ranks fifth among wide receivers, but missed time has limited his production and contributed to his drop in these rankings. He has yet to drop a pass this season, and his absence was certainly felt in the Eagles ' Monday Night Football loss.\n21. Garrett Wilson , New York Jets (down 2)\nThe emergence of Omar Cooper Jr. for the New York Jets has coincided with a couple of quiet games for the team's No. 1 receiver. Sunday's loss to the Miami Dolphins marked Wilson's least productive outing of the season, as he managed just two receptions for 26 yards on three targets across 29 receiving snaps.\n22. Jameson Williams , Detroit Lions (up 4)\nWilliams got off to a slow start this season, recording four drops over his first four games. He's turned things around over the past two weeks, however, racking up 136 receiving yards and four touchdowns. Those two games also featured his highest yards-per-reception averages of the season.\n23. Tre Tucker , Las Vegas Raiders (up 2)\nTucker has a strong case as the NFL's most underrated wide receiver right now. He ranks 22nd at the position with an average of 2.03 yards per route run and 16th with 474 receiving yards, while averaging 16.3 yards per catch. He's on track to enter free agency following a career year.\n24. Rome Odunze , Chicago Bears (no change)\nLuther Burden III has emerged as the Bears ' top target at wide receiver, but Odunze continues to make an impact. His 69 receiving yards against the New England Patriots on Thursday Night Football marked his second-highest total of the season.\n25. Jalen Coker , Carolina Panthers (down 5)\nThere weren't many bright spots for the Panthers offense in Sunday's loss to the Tampa Bay Buccaneers , and Coker was no exception. He finished with a season-low nine receiving yards despite running routes on 30 snaps.\n26. KC Concepcion , Cleveland Browns (unranked)\nConcepcion had shown flashes earlier in the season, but Sunday's game against the Tennessee Titans marked a true breakout for the Browns ' rookie receiver. Across 28 receiving snaps, he caught eight of 10 targets for 129 yards, setting career highs in targets, receptions, receiving yards, PFF receiving grade and yards per route run.\n27. Carnell Tate , Tennessee Titans (no change)\nTate leads all rookie wide receivers with 413 receiving yards, while his 2.00 yards per route run also leads rookies with at least 20 targets through seven weeks. He has faced just nine contested targets so far but has come down with eight of them.\n28. Khalil Shakir , Buffalo Bills (no change)\nShakir found the end zone for the first time this season in the Bills ' win over the Las Vegas Raiders , despite posting his second-lowest receiving yardage total of the year. His 5.6-yard average depth of target is the fifth-lowest at the position, highlighting his role in Buffalo's offense. He remains an excellent short-area target for the Bills coming out of their bye week.\n29. Keon Coleman , Buffalo Bills (no change)\nColeman appears to be enjoying a breakout season in his third NFL campaign and was a consistent big-play threat for the Bills before their bye week, averaging 14.9 yards per reception. His 77.9 PFF receiving grade ranks 19th among wide receivers, while his 1.93 yards per route run ranks 26th.\n30. Michael Wilson , Arizona Cardinals (up 1)\nThe Marvin Harrison Jr. trade hasn't led to a significant uptick in production for Wilson, but he remains a solid receiver, tied for 25th with a 73.4 PFF receiving grade. His efficiency numbers are more concerning, however, as his 1.32 yards per route run ranks tied for just 49th at the position.\n31. Devaughn Vele , New Orleans Saints (unranked)\nVele makes his second appearance at the back end of these rankings this season, putting together a solid campaign as New Orleans' No. 2 receiver while the team awaits Jordyn Tyson 's debut. His 73.1 PFF receiving grade ranks 27th among wide receivers with at least 20 targets.\n32. Denzel Boston , Cleveland Browns (down 2)\nAfter an impressive start to his NFL career, Boston has endured a quieter stretch over the past two weeks. He managed just one reception for 15 yards against the Baltimore Ravens in Week 6 before catching three passes for 27 yards in the win over the Tennessee Titans , a game in which he also recorded the first drop of his career.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-injury-report/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-injury-report/","url_hash":"c1a2e6acc1d06681f9259e1ee5f0cfe7864a67e9d34c2e05147b8a5da7935ed4","content_hash":"48c55359ded345323765444eec49c13da7e075e97c93b32cdba0bb0ba8aed631","title":"NFL Injury Report: Week 8","summary":"Stay up to date with the latest NFL injury report, including player injury statuses, actives and inactives ahead of game day. Follow live updates as player availability changes throughout the week and official game-day decisions are announced.","published_at":"2026-10-28T13:10:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-playoff-picture-2026/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-playoff-picture-2026/","url_hash":"8bbfc4b816ab54b1d56f075a2b29b562d458994620ce9b975b9bf59add0d15df","content_hash":"fdabc4deebadac6431bae58d338c852bc26beaa8f5a7d1799b851af7d73133a8","title":"2026 NFL playoff picture: Current standings, playoff probabilities and Week 8 leverage","summary":"The NFL playoff picture features the Kansas City Chiefs atop the AFC and the Minnesota Vikings leading the NFC, with both Minnesota and Buffalo able to clinch postseason berths with wins. The Cleveland Browns face the week's biggest playoff leverage, with their postseason probability ranging from 62.8% with a win to 27.4% with a loss.","published_at":"2026-10-28T13:00:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"PFF.com","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Chiefs and Vikings lead their conferences: Kansas City holds the top spot in the AFC playoff picture, while Minnesota leads the NFC. Both the Vikings and Buffalo Bills can clinch playoff berths with wins.\nBrowns face the biggest playoff leverage: Cleveland's postseason probability rises to 62.8% with a win but falls to 27.4% with a loss, a 35.4-percentage-point swing.\nRace for the No. 1 draft pick: The Tennessee Titans have a 32.7% chance of securing the first overall pick, compared with 32% for the Miami Dolphins .\nThe NFL playoff picture is taking shape, but the current standings tell only part of the story.\nWith another week of games that could shift the postseason outlook in both conferences, PFF’s playoff model highlights each team’s chances of reaching the playoffs, the games carrying the most leverage, and the teams best positioned to secure the No. 1 seed.\nAt the other end of the standings, the focus is already shifting toward the 2027 NFL Draft .\nNFL standings ahead of Week 8\nThe projections\nNaturally, the playoff picture is unlikely to stay the same for the rest of the season. And that’s why it makes sense to look at the projected playoff picture — the estimated chances PFF gives each team to make the playoffs.\nThe following charts display the projected playoff chances for each team, along with the playoff leverage, i.e., how the playoff chances would change with a win or a loss.\nThe logos show the current playoff chances. The points to the right of the logos indicate the chances after a win, and the points to the left indicate the chances after a loss.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-red-zone-report/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-red-zone-report/","url_hash":"3dfaced54963d9dd61a7a4db16ed97f74f45601087de73f7ffffa27f28b36aa3","content_hash":"13f6d5bfed7cb94f138e0784913e7edc9a43686c1788ce4dc49a238383e45f7f","title":"Fantasy Football Red-Zone Report: NFL Week 8","summary":"The Week 8 fantasy football red zone report tracks who is earning carries and targets inside the opponent's 20-yard line. It also shows which players are converting those opportunities into touchdowns and fantasy points.","published_at":"2026-10-28T12:55:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/10-nfl-players-outperforming-their-contracts-to-start-the-2026-season/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/10-nfl-players-outperforming-their-contracts-to-start-the-2026-season/","url_hash":"3833ae50fc79c545a03529dfbc55ae5c7573c5bf791a99d8a23f5cf3c270b60c","content_hash":"2ad660b3c9b4cdc51f6849f1143ee56451f4081ced64b0bb1f2107ff1f629b4b","title":"10 NFL players outperforming their contracts to start the 2026 season","summary":"As these 10 players exemplify, some of the NFL's best at each position are names making far lower than the average salaries.","published_at":"2026-10-28T11:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Bradley Locker","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Ventrell Miller headlines a pack of Jaguars : Miller owns the highest overall PFF grade among linebackers, while Parker Washington and Bhayshul Tuten have also made consistent contributions.\nDJ Reader looks like a steal: The new Giant is the league's top interior defender by overall PFF grade, dominating against both the pass and run.\nChristian Mahogany has been well worth his minimal contract: Despite owning a cap hit of slightly over $1 million this season, the third-year guard is one of the sport's best on an elite Lions offensive line.\nFor NFL teams to find broad success, they regularly need top-flight talent. In a majority of cases, that involves paying large sums of money to elite players — whether contract extensions for homegrown stars or top dollar to land big free agents.\nHowever, the sharpest organizations around the league make their dividends by finding excellent contributors on cheap contracts. From players on rookie deals leading their positional packs to unsung veterans on unassuming deals elevating themselves, these types of margins often prove significant.\nThrough seven games of 2026, these 10 players have performed like they’re worthy of tens of millions of dollars. But in reality, their contracts are serious bargains relative to what they’ve been afforded.\nNote: Players over a $10 million cap hit, in addition to rookies, were excluded from this exercise.\nLB Ventrell Miller , Jacksonville Jaguars\n2026 Cap Hit: $1.3 million\nThe highest-graded linebacker in football so far this year isn’t Fred Warner , Zack Baun or Jack Campbell . It’s Miller, who’s broken out in his first full season as a starter — placing as the highest-graded defensive player in the league.\nA fourth-round pick in the final season of his rookie deal, Miller has been the centerpiece of Jacksonville’s stingy defense. His 89.1 PFF coverage grade is the best among qualified linebackers, while Miller’s 90.6 PFF run-defense grade trails only Warner.\nIf his play extends throughout the second half of 2026, the 27-year-old looms as a potential All-Pro and likely extension candidate for the Jaguars this offseason after letting Devin Lloyd walk last spring.\nS Evan Williams , Green Bay Packers\n2026 Cap Hit: $1.28 million\nWilliams has consistently been an above-average safety over his first two seasons, but he’s turned things up a notch in 2026. His 90.2 overall PFF grade leads his position, as do his 0.06 Wins Above Replacement.\nWilliams has been a top-tier run defender for two straight years, but his coverage has improved by leaps and bounds in 2026. The third-year pro has permitted only 76 yards across 278 coverage snaps, allowing a 44.7 passer rating into his coverage while snagging two interceptions and dropping other potential attempts.\nThe Packers have needed other names to step up on their defense without Micah Parsons and Edgerrin Cooper , and Williams has been a certified star. The 25-year-old could receive a top-of-market extension in due time if his play sustains.\nC Zach Frazier , Pittsburgh Steelers\n2026 Cap Hit: $2.06 million\nThe upper tier of NFL centers has long revolved around Creed Humphrey , Tyler Linderbaum and even Aaron Brewer last season. However, that upper crust now has to include Frazier.\nThe Steelers’ anchor places as the league’s highest-graded center at a 78.0 mark. In pass protection, Frazier has yielded only seven pressures while giving up zero sacks. He’s been even better in the run game, adding a 78.9 PFF run-blocking grade while regularly opening lanes and climbing to the second level.\nMany thought Pittsburgh landed a future star in Frazier when it picked him in the second round two seasons ago. That time has now come, with Frazier powering one of the sport’s best offensive lines .\nEDGE Cameron Thomas , Atlanta Falcons\n2026 Cap Hit: $3.1 million\nThe season outlook for the Falcons was fairly dreary entering 2026, in large part due to a dearth of proven defensive talent. Yet newcomers like Thomas have put those concerns to bed.\nThe former Cardinal, Chief and Brown has found a home in Atlanta. His 76.6 PFF pass-rush grade is the 12 th -highest among qualified edge defenders, while his 25 pressures are tied for 14 th in that same group.\nConsistency is key for Thomas, who has mustered only six pressures over his last three games after a red-hot start. Nevertheless, he’s provided Atlanta with excellent returns for a player signed to a cheap, one-year deal, catalyzing the league’s fourth-highest-graded defensive line on a motley unit.\nQB Bryce Young , Carolina Panthers\n2026 Cap Hit: $5.93 million\nHeading into this season, the Panthers exercised the fifth-year option on Young but failed to reach a long-term contract extension. Now, Young is proving that he’s worth a substantial commitment.\nThrough the first seven games of the season, the former first overall pick owns the fourth-best overall PFF grade among qualified passers (82.4). Young has done an exceptional job taking care of the football, with his 1.1% turnover-worthy play rate the lowest in that contingent. On top of that, he’s made consistent plays under pressure, with his 62.2 overall grade under duress the sixth-best — all that despite facing the fifth-highest pressure rate (44.2%).\nGetting Young into a rhythm has been a multi-year challenge, and his poor Week 7 showing against the Buccaneers reinforces that concept. Nevertheless, the 25-year-old is playing well above his pay grade among quarterbacks, establishing himself as a franchise starter in Carolina — and one who could command over $50 million annually this spring.\nDl DJ Reader , New York Giants\n2026 Cap Hit: $4.5 million\nIn the aftermath of the Giants trading away star defensive lineman Dexter Lawrence , the team was left with a (literal) gaping hole up the middle of its defense. As it turns out, New York’s addition of Reader has been a ready answer.\nReader signed with the Giants in May on a two-year, $12.5 million contract but has played at a much loftier clip in 2026. More specifically, his 90.4 overall PFF grade is the best at the position — including a hair above Lawrence in second. Reader has only generated eight pressures but owns an elite 14.3% pass-rush win rate — the same as Jeffery Simmons this year — and his 12.6% run stop rate is also fifth.\nA player that’s proven he can play strong football in past seasons, Reader isn’t quite as much of a hidden gem compared to others on this list. Nevertheless, the 32-year-old is proving to be a very shrewd maneuver for the first-place Giants.\nWR Parker Washington , Jacksonville Jaguars\n2026 Cap Hit: $3.72 million\nThe Jaguars’ offense has ranked among the NFL’s most efficient since the start of 2025. While Trevor Lawrence ’s development has spearheaded the unit’s success, so too has the rise of Washington.\nWashington has played at a terrific caliber this season, vaulting himself into an unquestionably high discussion at his position. His 82.6 PFF receiving grade is the 10 th -best among qualified receivers, as is his 2.39 yards per route run. Not only is Washington a threat with the ball in his hands, averaging 4.8 yards after the catch per reception, but he’s elite against man or zone coverage — sitting in the top 13 in receiving grade when facing both schemes.\nA sixth-round pick in 2023, Washington has gone from neat story to the clear WR1 on one of the NFL’s top offenses. His performance should come with a new contract in short order.\nG Christian Mahogany , Detroit Lions\n2026 Cap Hit: $1.11 million\nMuch of the Lions’ downfall in 2025 was due to an offensive line that didn’t meet its typical standard of excellence. Yet the unit has returned to form in 2026, with Mahogany playing a leading role.\nMahogany’s 82.5 overall PFF grade is the sixth-best among qualified guards. His 3% pressure rate surrendered is the 11 th -lowest at his position, while his 9.6% positively graded play rate in the run game is second.\nMahogany’s career arc has been a sinuous one, featuring a tremendous start on a small sample size as a 2024 rookie to a middling 59.1 overall PFF grade as a full-time player in 2025. Now, the former sixth-round pick looks like a longtime cog in the NFL’s highest-graded offensive line, acting as another developmental success for Detroit.\nshows.\nPFF app : no CSS runs, but the card is built only from and ,\nwhich the app does not render - so only the appears.\nDo not convert any of the card's spans into or : that is exactly\nwhat leaks into the app. -->\nMONTHLY\nPFF Plus\nNEW - PRICE DROP!\n$9.99\nper month, billed monthly\nChoose Monthly\nPFF+ monthly, $9.99 per month\nWhat you unlock\nMOST FLEXIBLE\nPFF+ content and tools\nArticles, Fantasy tools, NFL and College tools, Betting\nAccess to mock draft simulator\nGuru AI football chatbot insights with limited tokens\nClick here to subscribe to PFF+ Monthly - $9.99 a month\nRB Bhayshul Tuten , Jacksonville Jaguars\n2026 Cap Hit: $1.3 million\nAkin to linebacker, running back was a position in which the Jaguars lacked clear-cut, reliable starters going into September. Tuten has answered the call and then some.\nThe second-year rusher has separated himself from a crowded Jacksonville committee, and for good reason. His 90.6 overall PFF grade is the best among running backs with 70 or more carries in 2026. Tuten has averaged an above-average 3.62 yards after contact per carry, and his 25 missed tackles forced tie for fifth.\nIn conjunction with Miller and Washington, the efforts of Tuten — a fourth-round pick in 2025 — help explain why the Jaguars have re maine d competitive this year despite suffering several personnel losses.\nRB Jaylen Warren , Pittsburgh Steelers\n2026 Cap Hit: $7.03 million\nWarren has long performed better than his contract has indicated. Even after a pay raise, he’s still transcending his financial value this season.\nWarren’s 82.6 overall PFF grade is sixth among qualified running backs. He sits third in PFF rushing grade at an 88.9 mark, averaging a robust 3.68 yards after contact per carry and ripping off 11 explosive runs that tie for fifth. He’s also been a major weapon in the Steelers’ short pass game, averaging 1.79 yards per route run with an extra 14.8 yards after the catch per reception.\nThe Steelers entrusted Warren to be their starting running back when they awarded him a two-year, $11.9 million contract extension in September 2025. Even so, he’s playing far superior than a back that ranks 16 th in cap hit and 27 th in average annual value— including lower than teammate Rico Dowdle .","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-fantasy-friendly-offenses-to-target/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-week-8-fantasy-friendly-offenses-to-target/","url_hash":"e5196577d91423394620dea319729568952d9e09c9d84f0c60b32e8b1c7319fd","content_hash":"2083b351f18a7b54a05285496d5466612e8a9bd38d22f8d3c0a706ed1314f93b","title":"Fantasy Football Week 8: Fantasy-friendly offenses to target","summary":"The Jacksonville Jaguars are among the most friendly offenses for fantasy football purposes entering Week 8 of the 2026 NFL season.","published_at":"2026-10-28T10:30:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nic Bodiford","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Buffalo Bills : Buffalo exits its Week 7 bye with the NFL’s top offense.\nJacksonville Jaguars : Jacksonville boasts the NFL’s most explosive passing offense.\nChicago Bears : Chicago leads the league in offensive plays per game.\nLeague-wide shifts toward heavy personnel packages are shrinking the fantasy football-viable player pool while widening the workload disparity between stars and rotational backups. NFL coaching staffs are also using fantasy-friendly tactics like shifts, motion and play action more often, leading to improved per-play results, particularly for the established starters who get to remain on the field all the time.\nFantasy managers can exploit these developments by identifying which NFL offenses will facilitate these high-end, highly efficient workloads.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-quarterback-rankings/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-quarterback-rankings/","url_hash":"26cb7b16d36974870cfb78e9180adf332f08a6fb49788580099138246c8c0251","content_hash":"df63a19543367adbb4a1a6391e890d3ef192d7669ea5287e346a7526b220fc8d","title":"NFL quarterback rankings: Joe Burrow leads the way, Dak Prescott surges","summary":"Bengals quarterback Joe Burrow takes over as PFF's No. 1 quarterback after a stellar Week 7 showing. Cowboys gunslinger Dak Prescott enters the top five, while Giants newcomer J.J. McCarthy shoots up after a strong first start.","published_at":"2026-10-28T10:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Joe Burrow makes for another swap at No. 1: Burrow's tremendous second half against the Ravens makes him the NFL's highest-graded passer in 2026.\nDak Prescott enters the top five: Prescott notched three big-time throws against the Eagles on Monday night, finishing with an 81.5-plus grade.\nJ.J. McCarthy places 21st: McCarthy notched a 79.8 PFF passing grade to lead the Giants to victory over the Texans in Week 7.\nshows.\nPFF app : no CSS runs, but the card is built only from and ,\nwhich the app does not render - so only the appears.\nDo not convert any of the card's spans into or : that is exactly\nwhat leaks into the app. -->\nANNUAL\nPFF Plus\nNEW - PRICE DROP!\n$99.99 $8.33 /mo\nper year, billed annually\nGo Annual\nPFF+ annual, $99.99 per year\nWhat you unlock\nBEST VALUE\nPFF+ content and tools\nArticles, Fantasy tools, NFL and College tools, Betting\nAccess to mock draft simulator\nGuru AI football chatbot insights with limited tokens\nPremium Stats\nMicro transactions available for additional tokens\nClick here to subscribe to PFF+ Annual - $99.99 a year (just $8.33 a month) billed annually\nWelcome to the weekly edition of PFF’s Quarterback Rankings. Unlike the grades in premium stats, these come with some priors built in, so are not simply a list of the highest graded players at the position.\nNow that we are in Week 8, there will be some more consideration for this season’s performance. There is more movement than in previous weeks, but I will try to not overreact too much to what is still a relatively small sample size.\nGiven the volume of injuries, any quarterback who is out for a significant amount of time will be excluded. Instead, their backup will be ranked.\n1. Joe Burrow , Cincinnati Bengals (up 1)\nBurrow was unstoppable in the second half of the Bengals’ win over the Baltimore Ravens in Week 7, leading the team back from a 13-0 deficit at the break to win 35-13. He paces all NFL quarterbacks with at least 100 dropbacks with an 89.5 PFF passing grade and ranks third with a 19.9% positively graded play rate while also owning the lowest negatively graded play rate at 5.4%.\n2. Brock Purdy , San Francisco 49ers (down 1)\nSunday’s loss to the Atlanta Falcons was Purdy’s worst game of the season, one that saw him produce a season-low zero big-time throws along with a season-high three turnover-worthy plays. However, his overall season numbers are still impressive, ranking sixth with a 19.2% positively graded play rate while adding the third-lowest negatively graded play rate at 6.0%.\n3. Josh Allen , Buffalo Bills (up 1)\nAllen jumps up one spot on the Bills’ bye week thanks to Patrick Mahomes dropping. While he has not been at his best as a passer this season, Allen has been able to keep the Bills’ offense churning with his legs. He leads all quarterbacks with a 91.5 PFF rushing grade, and his 21.2% positively graded play rate is 0.6 percentage points ahead of the next-best player at the position.\n4. Dak Prescott , Dallas Cowboys (up 5)\nAfter producing his lowest-graded performance of the season against the Green Bay Packers last week, Prescott bounced back with his second-highest-graded outing of the year against the Eagles on “Monday Night Football.” His 85.1 PFF passing grade ranks third among quarterbacks through the first seven weeks of the season.\n5. Jared Goff , Detroit Lions (no change)\nGoff’s 82.2 PFF passing grade is fourth in the NFL, but he’s coming off his lowest-graded game of the season. He entered the week with just one turnover worthy play in five games in 2026, but tallied four against the Green Bay Packers . He stays inside the top five with Patrick Mahomes falling, but that next group is hot on Goff’s heels.\n6. Drake Maye , New England Patriots (up 3)\nThe Patriots lost to the Chicago Bears on Thursday night, but Maye played his fourth straight game with a PFF passing grade above 77.0. He was outdueled by Caleb Williams but still went 24-for-31 for 321 yards. Maye’s 6.1% big-time throw rate leads all NFL quarterbacks this season.\n7. Patrick Mahomes , Kansas City Chiefs (down 4)\nThere are flaws in the Chiefs’ offense that extend well beyond Mahomes, but he has been unable to paper over the cracks quite like before. Sunday’s loss to the Seattle Seahawks was his lowest-graded game of the season, with Mahomes going just 19-for-33 for 165 yards with no big-time throws and two turnover-worthy plays.\n8. C.J. Stroud , Houston Texans (down 2)\nStroud is another quarterback coming off his worst game of the season, with the New York Giants ’ defense giving him a lot of trouble on Sunday. He threw for just 153 yards in the game, but on the season, he still ranks inside the top five in terms of both big-time throw and turnover-worthy play rates.\n9. Caleb Williams , Chicago Bears (up 2)\nWilliams just played his highest-graded game of the season, ranking second among all quarterbacks with an 83.4 PFF grade in Week 7. He has slotted inside the top-two players at the position in Accuracy Over Expectation in each of the past two weeks, and leads all quarterbacks with an 86.1% adjusted completion percentage through seven weeks.\n10. Bryce Young , Carolina Panthers (down 3)\nYoung ranks fifth among quarterbacks in terms of PFF grade so far this season, but his two lowest-graded performances of 2026 have come in each of the past two weeks. Over the first four games of the season, Young produced eight big-time throws to just one turnover-worthy play, but has two of each since Week 6.\n11. Jordan Love , Green Bay Packers (down 3)\nLove took a step back in Week 7 after three strong performances in the three weeks prior. After racking up six big time throws in each of the past two weeks, he finished with just one in the loss to the Detroit Lions , and matched that with a turnover-worthy play too. His 5.5% big-time throw rate this season ranks fourth at the position, but he also sports the third-highest negatively graded play rate.\n12. Trevor Lawrence , Jacksonville Jaguars (no change)\nLawrence stays at 12 on this list as the Jaguars return from their bye week. The pair of games in London were not kind to his PFF grade, with Lawrence’s 60.6 and 61.1 PFF grades in the two games overseas being his two lowest-graded outings of the season so far.\n13. Matthew Stafford , Los Angeles Rams (no change)\nStafford produced his second-highest-graded game of the season in the Rams’ win over the Las Vegas Raiders on Sunday, going 23-for-29 for 195 yards with three touchdowns and an interception. Despite that, his 70.5 PFF grade in the victory would have tied for his 15th-best game last season, so it’s clear he is not performing at the same level that saw him named NFL MVP a year ago.\n14. Jayden Daniels , Washington Commanders (up 1)\nDaniels moves up one spot despite the Commanders not playing this week thanks to Tyler Shough dropping down a couple of spots. Daniels has done a great job taking care of the football in terms of his turnover-worthy play rate, which ranks third among quarterbacks at 1.3%. However, he hasn’t found many big plays with his arm, with his 1.6% big-time throw rate ahead of only two other quarterbacks through the opening seven weeks of the season.\n15. Sam Darnold , Seattle Seahawks (up 2)\nDarnold was better in Week 7, earning a 72.7 PFF grade that ranked as his second-best mark of the season. He tossed a pair of big-time throws in the win over the Kansas City Chiefs , but also had a turnover-worthy play, with his 5.3% turnover-worthy play rate this season the fourth-worst in the NFL.\n16. Tyler Shough , New Orleans Saints (down 2)\nShough ended with his worst grade of the season at a 56.2 in the Saints’ loss to the Pittsburgh Steelers in Paris on Sunday. After having no big-time throws in the Saints’ previous two games, he generated a pair of them, but he also was culpable for two turnover-worthy plays — tied for the most he has had in a single game this season.\n17. Aaron Rodgers , Pittsburgh Steelers (up 2)\nRodgers has been at his best this season when he plays mistake-free football, and that’s the type of game we got from him in the win over the Saints in Paris. He has just two games this season where he has produced multiple big-time throws, but has played only one game where he has registered multiple turnover-worthy plays.\n18. Kyler Murray , Minnesota Vikings (up 1)\nMurray is not playing some sensational brand of football, with his 2.9% big-time throw rate tied for seventh-lowest in the NFL this season. But with the Vikings’ defense performing at an all-world level, his ability to take care of the football is enough. His 1.2% turnover-worthy play rate is the second-best mark in the league.\n19. Jacoby Brissett , Arizona Cardinals (down 3)\nBrissett has had a solid season for the Cardinals, but we saw his limitations on display in Arizona’s loss to the Denver Broncos on Sunday. With the Cardinals forced to pass to try to get themselves back into the game, he accrued three turnover-worthy plays as a passer — the most he has had in a game this season.\n20. Geno Smith , New York Jets (no change)\nSmith earned a PFF grade above 70.0 for the fourth time this season in Sunday’s loss to the previously winless Miami Dolphins . He is playing good, solid football this season. While he owns the fourth-lowest positively graded play rate at 11.9%, he also boasts the second-best negatively graded play rate at 5.8%.\n21. J.J. McCarthy , New York Giants (unranked)\nMcCarthy got the start for the Giants in their win over the Houston Texans on Sunday and has already proven to be a worthwhile acquisition for John Harbaugh’s men. He went 20-for-28 for 252 yards with three big-time throws and no turnover-worthy plays, garnering a +10.4% Completion Rate Over Expectation.\n22. Michael Penix Jr. , Atlanta Falcons (no change)\nWhile the underlying numbers don’t quite back up the raw stats for Penix this week, he did have a solid performance to help the Falcons stun the previously unbeaten San Francisco 49ers on Sunday. So far this season, he has nine touchdowns and just one interception, but he does own nine turnover-worthy plays.\n23. Cam Ward , Tennessee Titans (no change)\nWard was better than the raw stats suggest again this week, and there continue to be some positive signs for his development in Tennessee. After tallying three-turnover worthy plays against the New York Giants in Week 3, he has produced just three over the past four games.\n24. Bo Nix , Denver Broncos (up 1)\nNix played his second-best game of the season by PFF grade on Sunday in the win over the Cardinals, registering a 65.0 PFF grade in the game. He completed just 51.7% of his passes but pushed the ball downfield more with an 11.3-yard average depth of target that was a season high. He also more than doubled his season total in rushing yards on a 62-yard score in the game, finishing with 73 yards total.\n25. Kirk Cousins , Las Vegas Raiders (down 4)\nFor the first time this season, we have started to hear questions on whether or not the Raiders will turn to Fernando Mendoza at quarterback. Cousins hasn’t played badly, but his 9-to-7 big-time-throw to turnover-worthy-play ratio is less impressive than his 16-to-5 touchdown-to-interception ratio. Plus, four straight losses will always lead to questions around a change at the most important position in the sport when you have the No. 1 overall selection on the sidelines.\n26. Baker Mayfield , Tampa Bay Buccaneers (unranked)\nMayfield returned to the field for the Buccaneers this week but needed to throw just 11 passes given the level of blowout Tampa Bay inflicted on the Carolina Panthers . The good news was that he didn’t have a turnover-worthy play despite coming into the game with six on 119 dropbacks before his thumb injury.\n27. Deshaun Watson , Cleveland Brown s (down 3)\nWatson drops a few spots this week, with McCarthy’s impressive play in his first start and Mayfield’s return to the field the biggest contributors there. Week 7 marked Watson’s second-highest-graded game of the season, though he still ranks 28th out of the 34 NFL quarterbacks with at least 100 dropbacks with a 56.8 PFF grade in 2026..\n28. Malik Willis , Miami Dolphins (down 1)\nWillis drops down one spot but moves above Justin Herbert after producing his best performance of the year. He went 14-for-21 for 208 yards and a pair of touchdowns, and finished with no turnover-worthy plays in a game for just the second time this season.\n29. Justin Herbert , Los Angeles Chargers (down 3)\nHerbert falls three spots on his bye week to make room for Mayfield and McCarthy above him, and because Willis jumped ahead of him. He and the Chargers return from their break as the lone winless team in the NFL. Herbert ranks 34th among the 34 quarterbacks to have 100 or more dropbacks with a 44.5 PFF grade.\n30. Tanner McKee , Philadelphia Eagles (down 2)\nMcKee started his second straight game for the Eagles in the loss to the Dallas Cowboys on Monday night, and generally looked like a backup quarterback in the loss. He went 23-for-35 for 211 yards but finished with an average depth of target of just 5.6 yards.\n31. Daniel Jones , Indianapolis Colts (down 2)\nJustin Herbert is the only player within three-turnover plays of Jones this season, with the Colts’ signal-caller’s 6.2% turnover-worthy play rate dead last in the NFL and 0.4 percentage points higher than any other quarterback. There have been increasing calls for a change at the position in Indianapolis, but it looks like Jones will get the nod again against the Jacksonville Jaguars on Sunday.\nThe backups\n32. Tyler Huntley , Baltimore Ravens (down 1)\nDespite posting a career-high 298 passing yards against the Cincinnati Bengals , Huntley notched a sub-60.0 PFF grade for the second straight game. Through three-and-a-half games since taking over for the injured Lamar Jackson , Huntley has produced just one big-time throw, with his 0.9% big-time throw rate the worst mark in the NFL.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-offensive-line-rankings-2026/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-offensive-line-rankings-2026/","url_hash":"1bdb25022426559c2fce583c884aa5c8235ff699c53edc2ce808578a29840e6a","content_hash":"b04ccf32cb01e2d20bffcac8540008ea6c551691f64d8bcc9e1f9bea3f33116e","title":"2026 NFL offensive line rankings: Lions retain top spot, Colts fall without Quenton Nelson","summary":"The 2026 NFL offensive line rankings have been updated after Week 7, with the Detroit Lions retaining the top spot. The San Francisco 49ers and Pittsburgh Steelers round out the top three, while injuries and lineup changes affect units throughout the league.","published_at":"2026-10-28T09:45:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Gordon McGuinness","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"The Lions keep the No. 1 ranking going into Week 8: Detroit's pass protection was yet again sound against the Packers, including in the debut of Cade Mays.\nA 10-spot fall for the Colts: With All-Pro Quenton Nelson now in Seattle, Indianapolis will likely call upon Jalen Farmer at left guard.\nThe Patriots make a big step: An effective outing on Thursday against the Bears propels New England's injured group up 13 spots.\nThe offensive line is arguably the most important unit in football. The group, or even a single weak link within it, can derail a season quickly.\nThroughout the season, I will be ranking every offensive line in the NFL each week. That includes updating the list as we learn more about each player as the year wears on, and groups change due to injuries or players returning.\nHere are the updated PFF offensive line rankings after Week 7 of the NFL season. For all grades and metrics, including pass-blocking win rate, positively and negatively graded run-block rates, island rate and island win rate, subscribe to PFF Pro .\nMethodology\nTo score each offensive line, I first gave each player a grade between 5 and 10 in 0.5 increments, using their PFF grades over the past three years where applicable to shape that score, with a weighting toward pass protection . I then got an average for the group, with a slight weighting toward the left and right tackles to reach a mark out of 10 for each team in the league.\nOver the first four weeks of the season, I will only update the rankings based on lineup changes. That’s because the sample size in the first four weeks of the year is quite small. As we add each week and learn more about 2026 performance, we'll use that to increase or decrease each player's score.\n1. Detroit Lions (no change)\nLT: Penei Sewell\nLG: Christian Mahogany\nC: Cade Mays\nRG: Tate Ratledge\nRT: Blake Miller\nThe Lions did an excellent job in pass protection in the win over the Packers, with left tackle Penei Sewell and right guard Tate Ratledge allowing no pressures. Meanwhile, left guard Christian Mahogany permitted just one. Cade Mays surrendered two in his return to action at center, while rookie right tackle Blake Miller gave up three —  the second-highest pressure total in a single game of his career.\n2. San Francisco 49ers (no change)\nLT: Trent Williams\nLG: Connor Colby\nC: Jake Brendel\nRG: Dominick Puni\nRT: Colton McKivitz\nDespite the loss to the Atlanta Falcons , the 49ers’ offensive line remains high on our rankings. Left guard Connor Colby allowed five pressures and earned his lowest PFF grade of the season. The rest of the line combined to give up five pressures, with all four of them recording PFF pass-blocking grades above 70.0.\n3. Pittsburgh Steelers (up 4)\nLT: Troy Fautanu\nLG: Mason McCormick\nC: Zach Frazier\nRG: Brock Hoffman\nRT: Max Iheanachor\nZach Frazier now has five straight games with a PFF grade of 70.0 or better, and has a real chance of being a first-team All-Pro in 2026. The unit overall has just one starter with a PFF pass-blocking grade below 65.0, and that is right guard Brock Hoffman at 58.1. It’s a group without a real weak link and a strong duo on the interior in Frazier and left guard Mason McCormick .\n4. Buffalo Bills (no change)\nLT: Dion Dawkins\nLG: Alec Anderson\nC: Connor McGovern\nRG: O’Cyrus Torrence\nRT: Spencer Brown\nThe Bills were on a bye week in Week 7, but the lowest PFF pass-blocking grade among this group in Week 6 was a tie between both guards at 74.5, and that still tied for 16th at the position that week. Against the Raiders, Dion Dawkins was fantastic in pass protection again at left tackle, with no pressures allowed from 38 pass-blocking snaps.\n5. Denver Broncos (no change)\nLT: Garett Bolles\nLG: Ben Powers\nC: Luke Wattenberg\nRG: Quinn Meinerz\nRT: Mike McGlinchey\nThrough seven weeks of the season, three of the five starters on this offensive line have earned 70.0 or better PFF grades. Of the two who haven’t, right tackle Mike McGlinchey has at least been solid in pass protection with a 72.8 PFF pass-blocking grade, leaving left guard Ben Powers as the lone weak link here.\n6. Chicago Bears (no change)\nLT: Theo Benedet\nLG: Joe Thuney\nC: Garrett Bradbury\nRG: Jonah Jackson\nRT: Darnell Wright\nOf the 11 pressures surrendered by right guard Jonah Jackson , seven have come over the past three weeks, with him recording a sub-60.0 PFF pass-blocking grade in each. The good news this week was that right tackle Darnell Wright bounced back from the lowest-graded game of his season in Week 6 to his fifth game with a PFF grade above 75.0 this year in Week 7. Ozzy Trapilo has taken 35 snaps at left tackle over the past two weeks, and it wouldn’t be a shock to see him assume the starting job in the coming games, though Theo Benedet was solid in pass protection against the Patriots.\n7. Atlanta Falcons (up 3)\nLT: Jake Matthews\nLG: Matthew Bergeron\nC: Ryan Neuzil\nRG: Chris Lindstrom\nRT: Jawaan Taylor\nThe win over the San Francisco 49ers was this group’s best outing of the season, with left tackle Jake Matthews , left guard Matthew Bergeron and center Ryan Neuzil not allowing a single pressure in the game. Likewise, the right-side duo of Chris Lindstrom and Jawaan Taylor combined to let up three. \n8. Cincinnati Bengals (up 3)\nLT: Orlando Brown Jr.\nLG: Dylan Fairchild\nC: Ted Karras\nRG: Dalton Risner\nRT: Amarius Mims\nFor the fourth time this season, Bengals right tackle Amarius Mims did not allow a single sack, hit or hurry in pass protection in a game. His run-blocking brings his overall grade down, but he is the third-highest-graded right tackle in pass protection this season. Through six games, Mims has yielded just four pressures.\n9. Tampa Bay Buccaneers (down 1)\nLT: Tristan Wirfs\nLG: Ben Bredeson\nC: Graham Barton\nRG: Cody Mauch\nRT: Ben Chukwuma\nThe Buccaneers’ offensive line yielded just two pressures in the win over the Carolina Panthers , though it’s worth noting that Tampa Bay dropped back to pass just 14 times in the blowout win. This was Graham Barton ’s best game of the season overall and, after struggling at the start of the season, he has earned PFF grades above 65.0 each of the past two weeks.\n10. Los Angeles Rams (up 2)\nLT: Alaric Jackson\nLG: Steve Avila\nC: Coleman Shelton\nRG: Kevin Dotson\nRT: Warren McClendon Jr.\nThe Rams’ offensive line combined to allow two pressures in the win over the Las Vegas Raiders , and both of those were surrendered by right tackle Warren McClendon Jr. , with the rest of the line pitching a shutout. Jackson’s return from suspension gave Los Angeles a boost, with all five starters notching a PFF-pass blocking grade above 70.0.\n11. Dallas Cowboys (up 2)\nLT: Tyler Guyton\nLG: Tyler Smith\nC: Cooper Beebe\nRG: Tyler Booker\nRT: Terence Steele\nAll five of the Cowboys’ starters secured PFF grades above 70.0 this week in what was the best performance by an offensive line in Week 7. The line combined to allow just three pressures and power a rushing attack that went for 204 yards on the ground. Now that this group is fully healthy, it has the potential to be a top-five offensive line down the stretch.\n12. Seattle Seahawks (down 3)\nLT: Charles Cross\nRG: Quenton Nelson\nC: Jalen Sundell\nLG: Grey Zabel\nRT: Abraham Lucas\nThe Seahawks were set to drop by more here after what has been a tough start to the season for the interior of this offensive line. But then came the trade for Quenton Nelson on Tuesday. Nelson has earned a 32.6% PFF positively graded play rate on zone runs this season, which ranks third among all offensive linemen. The Seahawks have used a zone run concept 120 times this season, which trails only the Atlanta Falcons and Chicago Bears . The assumption would be that Zabel moves to right guard to make this work, but Nelson in this offense is a perfect fit.\n13. Indianapolis Colts (down 10)\nLT: Bernhard Raimann\nLG: Jalen Farmer\nC: Tanor Bortolini\nRG: Matt Goncalves\nRT: Jalen Travis\nThe Colts drop significantly here, and it’s all because of the trade of Quenton Nelson . The trade does make sense for a team that needs to rebuild, but in the short term, it leaves a huge hole at left guard. Nelson and left tackle Bernhard Raimann were the only two starters to record a PFF pass-blocking grade above 62.0 in the loss to the Minnesota Vikings .\n14. New England Patriots (up 13)\nLT: Will Campbell\nLG: Walter Rouse\nC: Jared Wilson\nRG: Matt Pryor\nRT: Morgan Moses\nThe Patriots dropped in recent weeks because of injury concerns, but they managed to navigate those absences well in the loss on Thursday to the Chicago Bears . All five starters finished with PFF grades above 60.0, including backup guards Matt Pryor and Walter Rouse .\n15. New York Jets (up 1)\nLT: Olu Fashanu\nLG: Jordan Meredith\nC: Josh Myers\nRG: Joe Tippmann\nRT: Armand Membou\nJosh Myers was the only starter in this group to secure a PFF grade below 60.0 in this game, but left tackle Olumuyiwa Fashanu was the lone starter to earn a PFF pass-blocking grade above 65.0. Indeed, all five starters on the line surrendered a pair of pressures in the loss to the Miami Dolphins .\n16. Cleveland Brown s (up 6)\nLT: Spencer Fano\nLG: Zion Johnson\nC: Elgton Jenkins\nRG: Austin Barber\nRT: Tytus Howard\nLeft tackle Spencer Fano had the best game of his young NFL career so far in the Browns’ win over the Tennessee Titans on Sunday. He allowed just two pressures from 41 pass-blocking snaps and collected a 92.4 PFF pass-blocking grade. The biggest worry for the Browns coming out of this game was right tackle Tytus Howard being evaluated for a concussion. If he can’t go on Sunday against the Pittsburgh Steelers , then Dawand Jones would step in at right tackle.\n17. New York Giants (no change)\nLT: Andrew Thomas\nLG: Jon Runyan\nC: Bryan Hudson\nRG: Francis Mauigoa\nRT: Jermaine Eluemunor\nThe Giants’ offensive line was solid this week, but given the defensive line it went up against in the win over the Houston Texans , that in itself is a big achievement. This was the best we’ve seen from rookie right guard Francis Mauigoa, with the first-round draft pick compiling an 87.8 PFF pass-blocking grade and giving up no pressures from 32 pass-blocking snaps.\n18. Minnesota Vikings (down 4)\nLT: Christian Darrisaw\nLG: Joe Huber\nC: Blake Brandel\nRG: Will Fries\nRT: Brian O’Neill\nThere were positives and negatives for the Vikings’ offensive line in their outing against the Colts this week. The trio of left tackle Christian Darrisaw , right tackle Brian O’Neill and left guard Joe Huber finished with PFF pass-blocking grades above 80.0. Center Blake Brandel and right guard Will Fries struggled on the other hand, with both falling under a 40.0.\n19. Las Vegas Raiders (down 4)\nLT: Kolton Miller\nLG: Spencer Burford\nC: Tyler Linderbaum\nRG: Caleb Rogers\nRT: DJ Glaze\nThis was a unit that was let down by both guard spots this week. Center Tyler Linderbaum bounced back from a tough performance to allow just one pressure from 53 pass-blocking snaps. Caleb Rogers at right guard had a game to forget, though, accruing a 28.6 PFF run-blocking grade and a 53.0 PFF pass-blocking grade.\n20. Washington Commanders (no change)\nLT: Brandon Coleman\nLG: Chris Paul\nC: Lucas Patrick\nRG: Sam Cosmi\nRT: Josh Conerly Jr.\nThe Commanders were on a bye week in Week 7, but should return the same starting lineup in Week 8. Right guard Sam Cosmi was the highest-graded player on this offensive line once again in Week 6, but the unit as a whole had some calamities in the loss to the San Francisco 49ers last Monday. Washington’s front five combined to permit 18 pressures, eight of which were either sacks or quarterback hits. Right tackle Josh Conerly Jr. surrendered six pressures on 51 pass-blocking snaps, a mark that led the team.\n21. Kansas City Chiefs (down 2)\nLT: Kahlil Benson\nLG: Kingsley Suamataia\nC: Creed Humphrey\nRG: Trey Smith\nRT: Joshua Ezeudu\nThere was some clarity about the left tackle position this week, with Josh Simmons heading to injured reserve due to a back injury. That means we won’t see him for at least another three games, but there is a strong possibility that he doesn’t play again this season. The Chiefs didn’t have a single offensive lineman earn a PFF grade above 60.0 in the loss to the Seattle Seahawks , and they are going to need better offensive line play to keep Patrick Mahomes upright when they take on the Denver Broncos on Sunday.\n22. Philadelphia Eagles (down 4)\nLT: Jordan Mailata\nLG: Landon Dickerson\nC: Drew Kendall\nRG: Tyler Steen\nRT: Markel Bell\nLandon Dickerson returned from injury to start at left guard on Monday night, but had trouble just as much as he did in Week 1 before heading to injured reserve. He allowed five pressures from 40 pass-blocking snaps in the loss to the Dallas Cowboys . Jordan Mailata had a tremendous performance at left tackle, relenting just one pressure, but this is a unit that looks like a shell of what we expected it to be at the start of the season.\n23. Tennessee Titans (no change)\nLT: Dan Moore Jr.\nLG: Peter Skoronski\nC: Austin Schlottmann\nRG: Atonio Mafi\nRT: JC Latham\nThe Titans’ offensive line is still in the bottom half of the league, but it is a group that is better than expected from the start of the season. A big part of that has been the development of JC Latham , with the starting right tackle earning an 80.3 PFF pass-blocking grade and allowing no pressures from 33 pass-blocking snaps in the loss to the Cleveland Brown s . The void in this group right now is still right guard Atonio Mafi ; in four starts this season, he has secured a PFF grade above 50.0 just once.\n24. New Orleans Saints (down 3)\nLT: Asim Richards\nLG: David Edwards\nC: Erik McCoy\nRG: Jeremiah Wright\nRT: Taliese Fuaga\nIt wasn’t a good day for the Saints’ offensive line in the loss to the Pittsburgh Steelers in Paris, and it really showed that the group had a pair of backups in the lineup. Left guard David Edwards and center Erik McCoy didn’t allow any pressure in the game, but the group as a whole didn’t have a single starter finish with a PFF grade above 60.0.\n25. Miami Dolphins (no change)\nLT: Patrick Paul\nLG: Kadyn Proctor\nC: Aaron Brewer\nRG: Jonah Savaiinaea\nRT: Austin Jackson\nIronically, the Dolphins’ first win of the season came in a game where their offensive line had some struggles, with both right guard Jonah Savaiinaea and right tackle Austin Jackson earning sub-45.0 PFF pass-blocking grades. The other three starters were solid enough in that regard though, while center Aaron Brewer collected Miami’s highest PFF run-blocking grade at 72.5.\n26. Carolina Panthers (no change)\nLT: Rasheed Walker\nLG: Damien Lewis\nC: Luke Fortner\nRG: Robert Hunt\nRT: Monroe Freeling\nThe Panthers would have been a bit higher here if not for the injury to Taylor Moton . The right tackle had just returned from injury but suffered what’s believed to be a season-ending injury in the loss to the Buccaneers on Sunday. That will push rookie Monroe Freeling back into the starting lineup. Left guard Damien Lewis is another player to watch on the injury report this week, with him landing on the injury report and the Panthers playing the Green Bay Packers on Thursday.\n27. Jacksonville Jaguars (up 2)\nLT: Walker Little\nLG: Emmanuel Pregnon\nC: Robert Hainsey\nRG: Ezra Cleveland\nRT: Cole Van Lane n\nThe Jaguars return from their bye having gotten a strong performance in pass protection from their starting tackles in Week 6, with Walker Little and Cole Van Lane n combining to let up just three pressures on 44 pass-blocking snaps in the loss to the Houston Texans . Jacksonville has had more woes on the interior, with rookie left guard Emmanuel Pregnon yet to finish a game with a PFF grade of 60.0 or better, and center Robert Hainsey ’s season-high PFF grade coming in that performance at just 61.7.\n28. Baltimore Ravens (down 4)\nLT: Ronnie Stanley\nLG: John Simpson\nC: Cam Jurgens\nRG: Vega Ioane\nRT: Roger Rosengarten\nThe Ravens’ offensive line had a tough day anyway, with no starters on the line registering a PFF grade above 60.0 in the loss to the Cincinnati Bengals , but it was made even worse by an injury to rookie right guard Vega Ioane. It’s not expected to be a long-term injury, but he will miss some time, and it pushed Emery Jones Jr. into the starting lineup. Jones has compiled a 44.1 PFF run-blocking grade and 44.2 PFF pass-blocking grade from 223 snaps this season.\n29. Green Bay Packers (up 2)\nLT: Jordan Morgan\nLG: Laken Tomlinson\nC: John Williams\nRG: Travis Glover\nRT: Anthony Belton\nLeft tackle Jordan Morgan and left guard Laken Tomlinson had solid days in pass protection, with both amassing PFF pass-blocking grades above 75.0. However, the right side of the line really had trouble, with right guard Travis Glover and right tackle Anthony Belton both mustering sub-45.0 PFF pass-blocking grades in defeat to the Detroit Lions . The Packers also lost center Sean Rhyan to a high ankle sprain in the game.\n30. Arizona Cardinals (no change)\nLT: Jayden Williams\nLG: Isaac Seumalo\nC: Hjalte Froholdt\nRG: Hayden Conner\nRT: Elijah Wilkinson\nCenter Hjalte Froholdt was the only player on the Cardinals’ offensive line to record a PFF pass-blocking grade above 60.0, coming in at 87.0 in the loss to the Denver Broncos . Rookie left tackle Jayden Williams has yet to find his footing since taking over from the injured Paris Johnson Jr. . Sunday was his worst outing of the season so far, with the seventh-round draft pick allowing eight pressures from 60 pass-blocking snaps.\n31. Houston Texans (down 2)\nLT: Aireontae Ersery\nLG: Keylan Rutledge\nC: Evan Brown\nRG: Ed Ingram\nRT: Trent Brown\nRookie left guard Keylan Rutledge has just one game this season where he has generated a PFF grade above 55.0, and this week saw him under a 40.0 for the second time this year. Through seven games, he has allowed 21 total pressures and played to a 34.1 PFF pass-blocking grade.\n32. Los Angeles Chargers (no change)\nLT: Travis Burke\nLG: Logan Taylor\nC: Jake Slaughter\nRG: Cole Strange\nRT: Alex Harkey\nGiven the volume of injuries on the offensive line for the Chargers, the group’s performance against the Kansas City Chiefs before their bye week was not that bad. Rookies Travis Burke and Jake Slaughter produced PFF pass-blocking grades above 70.0, with left tackle allowing three pressures from 49 pass-blocking snaps and the center one. Rookie right tackle Alex Harkey struggled, though, notching a 36.2 PFF pass blocking grade. It’s obvious that this group needs right tackle Joe Alt to return as soon as possible, and they could get both him and left guard Kayode Awosika back this week.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-wr-man-zone-coverage/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-wr-man-zone-coverage/","url_hash":"7adb8f21133a4029bb8d4ad679b68cfd8301fb80f9a1651b1f56c5b5a9402b67","content_hash":"5e41cbf6ea33e4c476c555c5f272154fc35d6700276e5caa30fb5eb8a2d9c552","title":"Fantasy Football Man, Zone Coverage Report: NFL Week 8","summary":"The fantasy football wide receiver report breaks down performance against man and zone coverage. It highlights wide receivers who have performed better against each coverage type and connects those splits to their opposing matchups.","published_at":"2026-10-28T09:30:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-idp-rankings/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-idp-rankings/","url_hash":"639175ea08910d613dbf925a488350edc768b7f6c87c2183bec76863ef9533a9","content_hash":"839c427b3201c8fff5eadd10cfad5e517b9b570c58f4f6165f384f962d88816f","title":"Fantasy Football IDP Rankings: Week 8","summary":"Nathan Jahnke's Week 8 fantasy football IDP rankings cover players across defensive positions for the 2026 NFL season.","published_at":"2026-10-28T09:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nathan Jahnke","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"David Bailey already ranks among the elite edge rushers: The second overall pick of the 2026 draft already ranks third in fantasy points per game among defensive linemen.\nAlex Singleton has been a tackling machine: The Denver Broncos linebacker has recorded 40 tackles over his last three games and is among the top linebacker options this week.\nMost accurate rankings: Nathan Jahnke currently ranks first in FantasyPros' IDP ranking competition after the first six weeks of the season and was first overall in Week 6.\nThese are my fantasy football rankings for the week for IDP leagues.\nThe table includes two positions: the PFF position, which you'll find in our premium stats, and the IDP position, which is the more traditional designation.\nInjury designations, such as Q for questionable, are included once they become official leading up to a game. Before then, the most recent practice status is provided, including DNP for did not participate, LP for limited participation and FP for full participation.\nYou can find rankings in other formats in our rankings tool . And for an even deeper look into the stats and trends for IDP leagues, check out our Fantasy Football IDP Report .","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-rankings-2026/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/fantasy-football-rankings-2026/","url_hash":"1b43484286bf29f66c00904b6fbf9e26da444b7a4fd626ff7a84334a569b87fd","content_hash":"2932c8fde9cd33f9e000ac62b25cf5dc63c75433176980b5940021d4bb680056","title":"Fantasy Football Rankings: NFL Week 8","summary":"Nathan Jahnke's Week 8 fantasy football rankings for the 2026 NFL season provide PPR lineup guidance and player evaluations.","published_at":"2026-10-27T20:15:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Nathan Jahnke","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Drake Maye returns as a top-five fantasy quarterback: Maye has a slow start to the season, but he’s played much better in recent weeks and has a favorable matchup against the Miami Dolphins defense in Week 8.\nOmar Cooper Jr. is already a must-start fantasy receiver: The New York Jets rookie has gained over 100 yards in every game he’s started and finished, and while it’s a small sample size, he’s still someone worthy of fantasy starting lineups this week.\nMost accurate rankings: Nathan Jahnke ’s fantasy rankings have been the most accurate in-season per FantasyPros ' rankings accuracy competition in each of the last six seasons among those who rank quarterbacks, running backs, wide receivers, tight ends, kickers, team defense and IDP.\nThese are my fantasy football rankings for the week for PPR leagues.\nAccording to FantasyPros , my rankings have been among the 20 most accurate in seven of the last eight seasons when considering only the core four positions, joining only three other analysts. I am one of three analysts to accomplish this, with no analyst finishing in the top-20 each of the last eight.\nInjury designations, such as Q for questionable, are included once they become official leading up to a game. Before then, the most recent practice status is provided, including DNP for did not participate, LP for limited participation and FP for full participation.\nYou can find rankings in other formats in our rankings tool .","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-seahawks-quenton-nelson-trade-analysis/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-seahawks-quenton-nelson-trade-analysis/","url_hash":"18e7f796524effa35da8ac37778bcac9cede185db96216779e965bc13c42f778","content_hash":"d9738514b40cbfa4b9167df945806cf327feed3163d2bc5869d54223ce9c5571","title":"Seahawks acquire All-Pro G Quenton Nelson from Colts for two draft picks","summary":"The Seattle Seahawks have agreed to acquire eight-time Pro Bowl guard Quenton Nelson from the Indianapolis Colts in exchange for two draft picks. Nelson brings an 85.7 PFF overall grade, an 81.9 pass-blocking grade and a league-leading 0.27 Wins Above Replacement among guards through seven weeks of the 2026 season. The trade could reshape Seattle's offensive line while opening a starting opportunity for Colts rookie Jalen Farmer.","published_at":"2026-10-27T20:12:53.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Seattle lands an elite guard: The Seahawks have agreed to acquire Quenton Nelson from the Colts for a 2027 third-round pick and a 2028 fifth-round pick. Nelson has earned an 85.7 PFF overall grade through seven weeks.\nMajor pass-protection upgrade: Nelson has allowed just six pressures across 275 pass-blocking snaps in 2026, with an 81.9 PFF pass-blocking grade and a 94.4% pass-block win rate.\nOffensive line changes ahead: Nelson's arrival could shift Grey Zabel to right guard and affect Anthony Bradford's role, while Indianapolis is set to turn to rookie Jalen Farmer at left guard.\nThe Seattle Seahawks are adding one of the NFL's most accomplished interior offensive linemen as they look to strengthen their offensive line for the remainder of the 2026 season.\nSeattle has agreed to acquire eight-time Pro Bowl guard Quenton Nelson from the Indianapolis Colts in exchange for a 2027 third-round pick and a 2028 fifth-round pick, according to ESPN's Adam Schefter and NFL Network's Ian Rapoport .\nNelson, 30, arrives in Seattle having earned an 85.7 PFF overall grade across 468 offensive snaps in 2026, continuing a stretch of high-level play that has seen him finish among the NFL's six highest-graded guards in each of the previous two seasons.\nThe move comes as Seattle looks to improve an offensive line that ranks 19th among 32 units in PFF pass-blocking grade (65.0). Through seven weeks, the group has combined to allow 15 sacks, 31 hits and 72 hurries. Seahawks quarterbacks have been sacked or hit on 18.2% of dropbacks, the fifth-highest rate in the NFL.\nParham\nLindstrom\nMeinerz\nCosmi\nBooker\nMahogany\nSmith\nJackson\nTippmann\nLewis\nMcCormick\nRisner\nSmith\nPuni\nRatledge\nGaines II\nAvila\nBergeron\nCleveland\nGoncalves\nTeller\nBarber\nIngram\nVera-Tucker\nSeumalo\nThuney\nSteen\nSkoronski\nAnderson\nJohnson\nProctor\nTomlinson\nBullock\nRuiz\nBass\nHoffman\nFries\nMeredith\nJackson\nTorrence\nCarmona\nColby\nSuamataia\nZabel\nHaynes\nEdwards\nHuber\nBredeson\nFairchild\nDotson\nAnderson\nBrown\nAdams\nMauch\nLampkin\nPowers\nPenning\nSimpson\nBurford\nBradford\nMonk\nSavaiinaea\nRunyan\nMauigoa\nGlover\nPregnon\nHunt\nPowers-Johnson\nBartch\nStephens\nStrange\nTaylor\nMafi\nPaul\nDickerson\nRutledge\nRogers\nJones Jr.\nJurgens\nNelson The same players and metrics are available as a sortable table on each team's Leaders tab.\nSource: Premium Stats Pro\nDylan Parham\nOG · NYJ\nRun Block Grade 90.4 1st / 80\nPass Block Grade 75.9 9th / 80\nChris Lindstrom\nOG · ATL\nRun Block Grade 85.7 6th / 80\nPass Block Grade 70.1 23rd / 80\nQuenton Nelson\nOG · IND\nRun Block Grade 82.3 7th / 80\nPass Block Grade 81.9 3rd / 80\nQuinn Meinerz\nOG · DEN\nRun Block Grade 86.7 4th / 80\nPass Block Grade 64.3 33rd / 80\nSam Cosmi\nOG · WAS\nRun Block Grade 80.2 10th / 80\nPass Block Grade 80.6 4th / 80\nTyler Bo