an Taylor\nOG · LAC\nRun Block Grade 44.8 73rd / 80\nPass Block Grade 50.3 57th / 80\nAtonio Mafi\nOG · TEN\nRun Block Grade 40.9 79th / 80\nPass Block Grade 53.9 53rd / 80\nChris Paul\nOG · WAS\nRun Block Grade 40.0 80th / 80\nPass Block Grade 58.8 46th / 80\nLandon Dickerson\nOG · PHI\nRun Block Grade 54.4 60th / 80\nPass Block Grade 21.9 80th / 80\nKeylan Rutledge\nOG · HST\nRun Block Grade 51.5 67th / 80\nPass Block Grade 34.1 74th / 80\nCaleb Rogers\nOG · LV\nRun Block Grade 50.0 72nd / 80\nPass Block Grade 33.6 75th / 80\nEmery Jones Jr.\nOG · BLT\nRun Block Grade 44.1 76th / 80\nPass Block Grade 44.2 65th / 80\nCam Jurgens\nOG · BLT\nRun Block Grade 50.5 71st / 80\nPass Block Grade 33.6 75th / 80\nNelson remains one of the NFL's top-performing left guards\nThe former No. 6 overall pick has continued to perform at a high level in both phases of the game in 2026, earning an 81.9 PFF pass-blocking grade and an 82.3 run-blocking grade. Those marks rank second and third, respectively, among qualifying left guards through seven weeks.\nIn pass protection , Nelson has surrendered just six pressures — including one quarterback hit and five hurries — across 275 pass-blocking snaps. His 2.2% pressure rate allowed ranks third among left guards and is less than half the 5.3% mark recorded by Seattle's current left guard, Grey Zabel .\nNelson also has the edge in pass protection , winning 94.4% of his pass-blocking reps to rank fifth, compared with Zabel's 91.7% win rate, which is tied for 22nd.\nThe Notre Dame standout has also gone 430 pass-blocking snaps without allowing a sack , a streak dating back to Week 14 of the 2025 season. It's hardly unfamiliar territory for the veteran guard, whose previous streaks include a remarkable 1,676 consecutive snaps without surrendering a sack, the fifth-longest such run in PFF history.\nBy comparison, Zabel has gone 108 pass-blocking snaps without allowing a sack, dating back to Week 5 of this season. His longest career streak stands at 451 snaps, a run that began and ended in 2025.\n2026 PFF grades: Quenton Nelson vs. Grey Zabel\nMetric\nQuenton Nelson\nGrey Zabel\nOffensive snaps\n468\n464\nOverall grade\n85.7\n59.8\nOverall rank\n2nd\n26th\nPass-blocking grade\n81.9\n64.5\nPass-blocking rank\n2nd\n20th\nRun-blocking grade\n82.3\n59.0\nRun-blocking rank\n3rd\n28th\nRank among left guards, Week 1-7\nThe difference is similarly apparent in the running game, where Nelson has earned positive grades on 22.3% of his run-blocking snaps (third) and negative grades on 12.2% (11th). Zabel, meanwhile, has recorded a 12.3% positively graded run-blocking rate (28th) and a 13.3% negatively graded rate (16th).\nNelson has been particularly effective on zone runs, earning a league-best 91.3 PFF run-blocking grade across 98 zone-blocking snaps. He has received positive grades on 32.6% of those plays, compared with a 15.8% negatively graded play rate.\nHis production has been less pronounced on gap runs, where he has earned a 64.3 run-blocking grade across 77 snaps.\nLindstrom\nNelson\nMeinerz\nCosmi\nBooker\nMahogany\nJackson\nTippmann\nLewis\nMcCormick\nRisner\nSmith\nPuni\nRatledge\nGaines II\nAvila\nBergeron\nCleveland\nGoncalves\nBarber\nIngram\nVera-Tucker\nSeumalo\nThuney\nSteen\nSkoronski\nAnderson\nJohnson\nProctor\nTomlinson\nRuiz\nBass\nHoffman\nFries\nMeredith\nTorrence\nColby\nSuamataia\nEdwards\nHuber\nBredeson\nFairchild\nDotson\nMauch\nPowers\nSimpson\nBurford\nSavaiinaea\nRunyan\nMauigoa\nGlover\nPregnon\nHunt\nPowers-Johnson\nStrange\nMafi\nPaul\nRutledge\nJurgens\nZabel The same players and metrics are available as a sortable table on each team's Leaders tab.\nSource: Premium Stats Pro\nChris Lindstrom\nOG · ATL\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 18.6% 12th / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 6.4% 1st / 60\nQuenton Nelson\nOG · IND\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 22.3% 4th / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 12.2% 18th / 60