 58th / 60\nJackson Powers-Johnson\nOG · LV\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 7.8% 58th / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 16.7% 35th / 60\nCole Strange\nOG · LAC\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 13.7% 33rd / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 18.0% 42nd / 60\nAtonio Mafi\nOG · TEN\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 6.7% 60th / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 24.4% 59th / 60\nChris Paul\nOG · WAS\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 9.9% 52nd / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 24.8% 60th / 60\nKeylan Rutledge\nOG · HST\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 12.1% 41st / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 16.6% 34th / 60\nCam Jurgens\nOG · BLT\nPositively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 10.4% 50th / 60\nNegatively Graded Run Block Rate · Graded Plays 22.6% 54th / 60\nNelson's arrival would likely trigger a reshuffling along Seattle's offensive line. The veteran has spent nearly his entire NFL career at left guard, logging 9,101 snaps at the position compared with just eight at left tackle. He has never played a snap at center, right guard or right tackle in the NFL.\nThat could leave Zabel as the more likely candidate to change positions. Although he primarily played left tackle at North Dakota State, Zabel has some experience at right guard.\nA move to right guard would also affect Anthony Bradford , who has held Seattle's starting job at the position since 2023. Bradford recently returned from injured reserve after missing five games with a knee injury and is in the final year of his rookie contract.\nBradford has struggled mightily in pass protection throughout his NFL career. He earned a 38.9 PFF pass-blocking grade across 419 snaps in 2023, followed by a 48.5 grade across 392 snaps in 2024 and a 32.2 grade across 661 snaps in 2025. Those struggles have continued into 2026, with Bradford posting a 22.9 pass-blocking grade across 60 snaps in two games. His continued difficulties could make him the odd man out if Seattle adds Nelson.\nThe Seahawks have not confirmed how they intend to align their starting offensive line following Nelson's arrival.\nthrough\n.\n-->\nDive into the data\nDive into the data\nPFF Blocking Report\n(opens in new tab)\n→\nQuenton Nelson 's PFF career grades\nNelson has consistently ranked among the NFL's highest-graded guards since entering the league, earning an overall grade of at least 79.0 in six of his first eight seasons.\nHis best season came in 2019, when he earned a 91.2 PFF overall grade, second among 89 qualifying guards. More recently, Nelson earned an 81.3 grade in 2024 and an 84.7 mark in 2025, ranking sixth and fourth at the position, respectively.\nQuenton Nelson: PFF grades and rank since 2018\nSeason\nSnaps\nOffense\nRank\n2018\n1136\n79.7\n5 of 88\n2019\n1044\n91.2\n2 of 89\n2020\n1082\n86.2\n4 of 92\n2021\n767\n69.1\n26 of 90\n2022\n1148\n68.4\n21 of 87\n2023\n1141\n70.8\n15 of 88\n2024\n1083\n81.3\n6 of 83\n2025\n1039\n84.7\n4 of 87\n2026\n474\n86.4\n5 of 119\nNelson's value extends beyond his PFF grades\nNelson's value is also reflected in PFF's Wins Above Replacement (WAR) metric, which measures a player's contribution relative to a replacement-level player.\nThe veteran guard has generated 0.27 WAR through the first seven weeks of the 2026 season, ranking first among 97 qualifying guards. That continues a career-long trend of high-level production, with Nelson leading all guards in WAR in five of his nine NFL seasons.\nHe ranked first at the position in each of his first three seasons, producing 0.48 WAR in 2018 and 0.58 in both 2019 and 2020. Nelson returned to the top of the position in 2024 with 0.26 WAR before finishing third among 138 guards with 0.55 WAR in 2025.\nWins Above Replacement\nQuenton Nelson · G\nPlayer WAR\nPositional avg\nSelect a season for WAR rank\nAvg G (200+ snaps)\nColts turn to rookie Jalen Farmer\nIndianapolis, meanwhile, is set to replace Nelson with rookie guard Jalen Farmer , whom the team selected out of Kentucky in the fourth round of the 2026 NFL Draft with the No. 113 overall pick.\nFarmer played exclusively at right guard during his college career, meaning the move into Nelson's former position will represent a change in alignment.\nIn his final season at Kentucky , Farmer earned a 69.8 PFF overall grade, including a 72.4 pass-blocking grade and a 67.4 run-blocking grade across 818 offensive snaps.\nHe surrendered 14 pressures, including three sacks, across 457 pass-blocking snaps in 2025.\nJalen Farmer: College pass-blocking snaps\nCollege season\nSchool\nOffensive snaps\nOverall grade\nPass-blocking grade\nRun-blocking grade\n2022\nFlorida\n29\n79.9\n80.7\n80.2\n2023\nFlorida\n27\n71.5\n80.1\n70.4\n2024\nKentucky\n765\n62.3\n68.2\n61.1\n2025\nKentucky\n818\n69.8\n72.4\n67.4\nNelson departs Indianapolis in the final year of his contract, which carries a $16 million base salary in 2026. The Colts did not reach an extension agreement with him before the season.\nFor Seattle, the trade adds one of the league's highest-graded left guards to an offensive line dealing with performance concerns and injuries, including an ankle injury to left tackle Charles Cross .\nFor Indiana polis, it marks the end of Nelson's spectacular tenure with the franchise and opens the door for Farmer to take over at left guard.","revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-coverage-matchup-tracker/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-coverage-matchup-tracker/","url_hash":"196ca74fae44c71ad12ca35dfe8a4ba234a8525990fa78bccf8527f4910f9d51","content_hash":"7e3a5abaf48a3b45bf96952c3265c802104d563575eeab1ffa5ae7a01dd43a64","title":"Coverage Matchup Tracker: NFL Week 8","summary":"The PFF Matchup Tracker is an interactive NFL coverage matchup tracker that compares receivers, tight ends and running backs against cornerbacks, safeties and linebackers. Powered by PFF Premium Stats, the tool provides head-to-head coverage statistics dating back to 2006, including targets, catches, yards, touchdowns, interceptions and passer rating.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:55:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-receiver-drop-stats-streaks/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-receiver-drop-stats-streaks/","url_hash":"4fb04ecd16f5b720054bd94f1254b1dcef66e0ff84b039cd6691623fe27c095a","content_hash":"5bef10aaf8027b61430a3fc916428396c1d0787f9b935a27ff5761ad6d768393","title":"NFL Receiver Drop Stats &#038; Streaks: NFL Week 8","summary":"The PFF Drop-Free Streak Tracker measures how many consecutive catchable passes NFL receivers have caught without a drop. Powered by PFF Premium Stats, the interactive tool features active streaks, historical results dating back to 2017 and position filters.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:50:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-wr-te-separation-report/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-wr-te-separation-report/","url_hash":"bba9ab42da85da239ee668136b4d6aaddd2691e408c348e0e3d58d7b6345b1c5","content_hash":"0f8ffdf3340e3f7308a3d7737d774481d74df8fd48076edfeefe55182f9fd0d7","title":"NFL WR/TE Separation Report: NFL Week 8","summary":"The NFL WR/TE Separation Report analyzes how frequently wide receivers and tight ends are open, measuring separation percentages across targets. The tool breaks down route types, throw types and quarterback reads.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:40:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-qb-receiver-partnerships/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-qb-receiver-partnerships/","url_hash":"d49dd9bc140b4169970dab940125c34b45b329547184fddb4b169bf1c293fd12","content_hash":"dafaa66c473adc0884414924c6466ab6b1ccb1facbf119d43438e323df418613","title":"The QB-WR Partnership Report: NFL Week 8","summary":"Explore NFL QB-receiver partnerships and compare individual passing connections across the league. The tool provides data for quarterback and receiver combinations, allowing users to examine how different partnerships perform.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:30:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-pass-protection-tracker/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-pass-protection-tracker/","url_hash":"352ec1a2e811276d2adf3f56d0e8eb4c025891ba918b643fb3470dfae63e917b","content_hash":"fc72c00b8ae6891faf0adc645b05b357b050746af25a6ad5c2e7f61a75148bfe","title":"PFF Pass Protection Tracker: NFL Week 8","summary":"Track the NFL’s longest pass-protection streaks and see which offensive linemen have gone the most consecutive pass-blocking snaps without allowing a sack, hit or pressure. Explore active streaks and all-time leaders using PFF data dating back to 2006.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:25:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"PFF","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-qb-accuracy-report/","canonical_url":"https://www.pff.com/news/nfl-qb-accuracy-report/","url_hash":"34295012812bbf3ba03fc6d4b94c4a8e71394a7196dae4f48bcbfff7dbfa5b47","content_hash":"47ad21a9967066ae20597992da0d60bf7fc8db20f2c0b06ac5db7668cb3fa30f","title":"QB Accuracy Report: NFL Week 8","summary":"The PFF QB Accuracy Index ranks NFL quarterbacks using advanced accuracy metrics, including CPOE, ACCOE and adjusted completion percentage. Explore how quarterbacks compare across PFF Grades, EPA per play and other passing metrics.","published_at":"2026-10-27T16:20:00.000Z","confidence":0.67,"author":"Mark Chichester","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":null,"revision_hash":null},{"source":"Pro Football Talk","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/rumor-mill/news/jon-grudens-case-against-the-nfl-isnt-going-well-and-its-his-own-fault","canonical_url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/rumor-mill/news/jon-grudens-case-against-the-nfl-isnt-going-well-and-its-his-own-fault","url_hash":"54dadb11f629316618965c3fcbccee1469c1a1c9ab332622bcefec4b8294b6f2","content_hash":"4c75a2bb548121fc50c42ecbeef275fce219552dce56b7b9ed5650d57a90b701","title":"Jon Gruden's case against the NFL isn't going well — and it's his own fault","summary":"Said the presiding judge in a recent hearing: “We have no reason to trust representations by Mr. Gruden.\"","published_at":"2026-10-29T14:22:51.000Z","confidence":0.66,"author":"Mike Florio","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Jon Gruden's case against the NFL isn't going well — and it's his own fault - NBC Sports\nSkip navigation\nPro Football Talk\nRumor Mill\nPFT Video\nPFT PM\nPFT Live\nPFT Clips\nPFT Draft: Best matchups of Week 8\nWhy questions swirl around Lamar’s health\nCoen trying to keep Jaguars focused vs. Colts\nOther PFT Content\nChristian Watson, Matthew Golden are delivering for the Packers\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nKirk Cousins is having a rare debut run with the Raiders\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nDeion Sanders dismisses “a few guys” from the team after blowout loss in Northwestern\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nPro Football Talk\nRumor Mill\nPFT Video\nPFT PM\nPFT Live\nPFT Clips\nPFT Draft: Best matchups of Week 8\nWhy questions swirl around Lamar’s health\nCoen trying to keep Jaguars focused vs. Colts\nOther PFT Content\nChristian Watson, Matthew Golden are delivering for the Packers\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nKirk Cousins is having a rare debut run with the Raiders\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nDeion Sanders dismisses “a few guys” from the team after blowout loss in Northwestern\nMike Florio ,\nMike Florio ,\nProfile\nLogin or Create Profile\nFavorites\nMenu\nFavorites\nSign up to follow your favorites on all your devices.\nSign up\nTeams\nAFC East\nBuffalo Bills\nMiami Dolphins\nNew England Patriots\nNew York Jets\nAFC North\nBaltimore Ravens\nCincinnati Bengals\nCleveland Browns\nPittsburgh Steelers\nAFC South\nHouston Texans\nIndianapolis Colts\nJacksonville Jaguars\nTennessee Titans\nAFC West\nDenver Broncos\nKansas City Chiefs\nLas Vegas Raiders\nLos Angeles Chargers\nNFC East\nDallas Cowboys\nNew York Giants\nPhiladelphia Eagles\nWashington Commanders\nNFC North\nChicago Bears\nDetroit Lions\nGreen Bay Packers\nMinnesota Vikings\nNFC South\nAtlanta Falcons\nCarolina Panthers\nNew Orleans Saints\nTampa Bay Buccaneers\nNFC West\nArizona Cardinals\nLos Angeles Rams\nSan Francisco 49ers\nSeattle Seahawks\nAFC East\nBuffalo Bills\nMiami Dolphins\nNew England Patriots\nNew York Jets\nAFC North\nBaltimore Ravens\nCincinnati Bengals\nCleveland Browns\nPittsburgh Steelers\nAFC South\nHouston Texans\nIndianapolis Colts\nJacksonville Jaguars\nTennessee Titans\nAFC West\nDenver Broncos\nKansas City Chiefs\nLas Vegas Raiders\nLos Angeles Chargers\nNFC East\nDallas Cowboys\nNew York Giants\nPhiladelphia Eagles\nWashington Commanders\nNFC North\nChicago Bears\nDetroit Lions\nGreen Bay Packers\nMinnesota Vikings\nNFC South\nAtlanta Falcons\nCarolina Panthers\nNew Orleans Saints\nTampa Bay Buccaneers\nNFC West\nArizona Cardinals\nLos Angeles Rams\nSan Francisco 49ers\nSeattle Seahawks\nRumor Mill\nPeacock\nPFT Shop\nJon Gruden’s case against the NFL isn’t going well — and it’s his own fault\nBy\nMike Florio ,\nBy\nMike Florio\nPublished October 29, 2026 10:22 AM\nFacebook\nTwitter\nEmail\nJon Gruden asked for it, and Jon Gruden definitely is getting it.\nAfter a legal odyssey that somehow staved off the NFL’s obsession to force his five-year-old lawsuit into arbitration controlled by the league, Gruden won the ability to have his claims litigated in open court. Now that the litigation is moving forward, Gruden is drowning in the obligations of the discovery process.\nIn August, the presiding judge publicly criticized Gruden and his lawyers for “ lack of compliance with the rules .” More recently, it got worse for Gruden.\nA lot worse.\nMichael McCann of Sportico has obtained a transcript of a recent hearing regarding the ongoing efforts of the NFL to secure information to which the league is entitled, under the so-called rules of civil procedure. And the transcript sends an unmistakable message: Judge Joe Hardy seems to suspect that Gruden could be actively hiding evidence that could harm his case.\nThe current focal point is a phantom BlackBerry device. It existed, and now it doesn’t.\n“ So did your client lie to you about a BlackBerry?” Judge Hardy said at one point during the hearing.\n“ It’s not a lie, Your Honor, it’s a mistake,” attorney Adam Hosmer-Henner responded. “And what [Gruden] told me is that he had an old BlackBerry that he couldn’t turn on.”\nThe judge apparently senses that Gruden’s side is playing a shell game with the BlackBerry device in question.\n“Did anybody from your office ever check to see if there was an actual BlackBerry?” Judge Hardy asked Hosmer-Henner.\n“Mr. Gruden resides in Florida,” Hosmer-Henner said. “We did not send an individual to rummage around his house to look for a BlackBerry. And what we did do is promptly respond to interrogatory responses providing all this information. With regard to the forensic collection, Mr. Gruden was at an event at the White House last week. He flew back that night in order to allow the forensic examiners selected by the NFL to go through his electronic devices and —\"\n“So let me interject there, too, because if you can’t tell, this process is frustrating,” Judge Hardy said. “And you, on behalf of your client, keep saying things that seem to indicate, well, Mr. Gruden is super important and super special. And, you know, we should accommodate his schedule. For example, you just mentioned, well, he was at an event at the White House.”\nAt one point during the hearing, Judge Hardy said this: “We have no reason to trust representations by Mr. Gruden.”\nJudge Hardy, per McCann, ordered Gruden to compensate the league for a portion of the attorneys’ fees incurred in connection with the ongoing effort to force him to comply with the rules. That’s an unusual development in any civil case, and it underscores Judge Hardy’s growing frustration with Gruden and his lawyers.\nHere’s why it’s a major problem for Gruden. The same judge who has come to the conclusion that the “representations by Mr. Gruden” should not be trusted will be making many key decisions about the merits of Gruden’s case. If it proceeds to trial, Gruden will testify under the supervision of Judge Hardy.\nAnd Gruden, who has a strong will, a quick temper, and a profane vocabulary, will be scrutinized very carefully by a judge who has already decided that he has “no reason to trust representations by Mr. Gruden.”\nSo, no, it’s not going well for Gruden. And it’s his own fault. At best, he has failed to honor the duty to comply with the rules of the legal process he initiated. At worst — and as the judge undoubtedly suspects — Gruden possibly is trying to hide a device that may contain communications that could hurt, if not kill, his entire case.\nOn the surface, the idea that someone connected to the NFL weaponized supposedly confidential emails from an unrelated investigation to force Gruden out of his head-coaching job with the Raiders during the 2021 season is troubling. It becomes impossible for Gruden (who is already operating at a disadvantage due to the objectively problematic contents of the emails that were leaked) to prove a violation of applicable legal principles if he creates the impression that he’s not cooperating with the process.\nOr, even worse, if he is deliberately and actively trying to conceal other emails and/or text messages.\nAt this point, it becomes critical for Gruden to do anything and everything he can to regain the trust of the judge. Without that, it’s just a matter of time before Gruden loses his case — and possibly ends up being required to reimburse the NFL for millions of dollars in fees and expenses incurred in defending itself against a case that Gruden seems to not be taking as seriously as he should.\nClose Ad\nAdd favorite players, teams, and leagues with an NBCUniversal Profile\nContinue","revision_hash":null},{"source":"Pro Football Talk","source_type":"analytical","url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/rumor-mill/news/zay-flowers-you-know-lamar-jackson-he-wants-to-play","canonical_url":"https://www.nbcsports.com/nfl/profootballtalk/rumor-mill/news/zay-flowers-you-know-lamar-jackson-he-wants-to-play","url_hash":"d93e4a592e2747bcfe279b3d0b1c61646be2e369ecc338e757684869aca80319","content_hash":"ae8c2e4a4fe6b9d7f971bcd67445c3ad043dde81d72b39a195bedb6825aa31db","title":"Zay Flowers: You know Lamar Jackson, he wants to play","summary":"Ravens quarterback Lamar Jackson was officially a full participant in Wednesday's practice, which ostensibly puts him on course to start Sunday's game against the Bills.","published_at":"2026-10-29T14:06:27.000Z","confidence":0.66,"author":"Myles Simmons","captured_at":"2026-10-29T14:59:12.476Z","body":"Zay Flowers: You know Lamar Jackson, he wants to play - NBC Sports\nSkip navigation\nPro Football Talk\nRumor Mill\nPFT Video\nPFT PM\nPFT Live\nPFT Clips\nPFT Draft: Best matchups of Week 8\nWhy questions swirl around Lamar’s health\nCoen trying to keep Jaguars focused vs. Colts\nOther PFT Content\n