st\nArizona Cardinals\nLos Angeles Rams\nSan Francisco 49ers\nSeattle Seahawks\nAFC East\nBuffalo Bills\nMiami Dolphins\nNew England Patriots\nNew York Jets\nAFC North\nBaltimore Ravens\nCincinnati Bengals\nCleveland Browns\nPittsburgh Steelers\nAFC South\nHouston Texans\nIndianapolis Colts\nJacksonville Jaguars\nTennessee Titans\nAFC West\nDenver Broncos\nKansas City Chiefs\nLas Vegas Raiders\nLos Angeles Chargers\nNFC East\nDallas Cowboys\nNew York Giants\nPhiladelphia Eagles\nWashington Commanders\nNFC North\nChicago Bears\nDetroit Lions\nGreen Bay Packers\nMinnesota Vikings\nNFC South\nAtlanta Falcons\nCarolina Panthers\nNew Orleans Saints\nTampa Bay Buccaneers\nNFC West\nArizona Cardinals\nLos Angeles Rams\nSan Francisco 49ers\nSeattle Seahawks\nRumor Mill\nPeacock\nPFT Shop\nMike McDaniel will remain the Chargers’ offensive playcaller\nBy\nJosh Alper ,\nBy\nJosh Alper\nPublished October 28, 2026 03:33 PM\nFacebook\nTwitter\nEmail\nChargers head coach Jim Harbaugh said that the team would spend their bye week “ looking into everything ” when it came to improving their offense and did not rule out a change involving offensive coordinator Mike McDaniel.\nMcDaniel was not dismissed and he hasn’t lost one of his primary job responsibilities either. Harbaugh confirmed at a press conference on Wednesday that McDaniel will continue to call the team’s offensive plays when they return to action against the Rams on Sunday.\nMcDaniel’s future was in doubt because the offense was a disaster over the course of the team’s 0-6 start. They are averaging a league-worst 15 points per game while turning the ball over more frequently than any of the other 31 teams, so there’s a lot of room for improvement once the team is back on the field.\nThe Rams aren’t the easiest defense to face when you’re trying to get an offense on track, but that’s the challenge McDaniel will have to meet for the Chargers to find their first win of the season.\nClose Ad\nAdd favorite players, teams, and 