},{\"row\":7,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":7,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":7,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":7,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":7,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":7,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":7,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":7,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":8,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":8,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":8,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":8,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":8,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":9,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":9,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":9,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":9,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":9,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":10,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":10,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":10,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":10,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":10,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":11,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":11,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":11,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":11,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":11,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":12,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":12,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nPhiladelphia Rank @ Washington Rank\n4.5 Spread -4.5\n18.0 Implied Total 22.5\n18.1 29 Points/Gm 22.0 20\n25.0 21 Points All./Gm 28.7 31\n58.9 27 Plays/Gm 63.5 12\n67.3 29 Opp. Plays/Gm 63.5 20\n4.5 32 Off. Yards/Play 5.1 21\n5.5 19 Def. Yards/Play 5.6 22\n38.6% 25 Rush% 43.3% 18\n61.4% 8 Pass% 56.7% 15\n46.5% 26 Opp. Rush % 39.6% 7\n53.5% 7 Opp. Pass % 60.4% 26\nThe Eagles are last in the NFL in plays of 20-plus yards per game (2.0).\nThe Eagles have scored on 28.8% of their possessions, 31st in the league.\nWashington has allowed a league-high 81.8% touchdown rate on red-zone trips (18 of 22).\nThe Commanders have turned the ball over on 2.9% of their possessions, the lowest rate in the league.\nWashington has converted a league-low 30.9% (25 of 81) of its third downs.\nThe Eagles average 8.4 yards needed per third down, the most in the league.\nWashington averages 8.1 yards needed per third down, 31st.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nEagles vs. Commanders Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nJayden Daniels\nSince returning to the lineup, Daniels has been the QB2 (32.3 points) and QB6 (21 points).\nIn Week 5, he did his damage as a runner, rushing for 22.3 points.\nIn Week 6, he ran for only 10 yards but threw for 300 yards and 2 touchdowns.\nThat was the second 300-yard passing game of his career.\nThe marriage of rushing and passing upside is what made Daniels so good in his rookie season.\nThat is what makes Daniels an upside QB1.\nDaniels has averaged 8.0 Y/A and 7.5 Y/A in his two starts since injury.\nWhen these teams met in Week 1, it wasn't pretty in a tough matchup, but Daniels still turned in a serviceable fantasy game.\nHe closed the week as the QB13 (17.7 points).\nDaniels completed 18 of 34 passes (52.9%) for 164 yards and 4.8 yards per pass attempt.\nHe threw 2 touchdowns while adding 31 rushing yards to anchor a poor passing day.\nSince the opener, the Eagles have trended in the wrong direction.\nThey have allowed three straight QB1 scoring weeks and top-12 weeks to four of the past five passers they have faced.\nThe Eagles still allow only a 59.8% completion rate (4th).\nDak Prescott (55.6%) and Bryce Young (51.3%) were inefficient in completing passes against the Eagles over the past two weeks.\nBut both, and previous quarterbacks, have connected on explosive plays and touchdowns.\nWhen the Eagles allow completed passes, they give up 11.9 yards per completion (27th).\nThey allow a 4.5% touchdown rate (19th), with multiple passing touchdowns in five games.\nOne of this defense's biggest issues (other than being tied to the offense) is that they aren't getting to the quarterback.\nThe Eagles are 26th in pressure rate (26th) and last in the NFL in quick pressure rate (8.9%)\nThey are last in the NFL in sack rate (3.2%).\nWhen the Eagles have gotten pressure, they have allowed a league-low 32.8% completion rate, 4.8 yards per pass attempt (7th), and a 0% touchdown rate.\nWithout pressure, they are allowing 7.9 Y/A (24th) and a 6.1% touchdown rate (24th).\nThat was the story in Week 1.\nTheir best game this season for creating pressure was Week 1 against this offense (40%).\nWhen the Eagles pressured Daniels in Week 1, he threw 2 of 12 passes for 17 yards (1.4 Y/A).\nDaniels' other avenue to score points when the pressure comes and passing is muted is his legs.\nThe Eagles are 26th in rushing points allowed to quarterbacks this season (3.9 per game).\nTanner McKee\nMcKee has finished as the QB18 (12.6 points) and QB22 (10.5 points) in his two starts.\nThe Eagles are so banged up offensively that you can give McKee some leeway, but it is ultimately hard to use him as more than back-end QB2 on needy 2QB rosters.\nWe did see gradual changes in the offense during McKee's second start, with a game plan built around him.\nThe Eagles went from a 21.3% rate under center in Week 6 up to 33.9% last week.\nThat allowed them to raise their play-action rate from 11.1% in Week 6 to 22.9% on Monday.\nThey went from a 43.8% rate of pre-snap motion to 52.6%.\nThose are still bottom-end rates, but there was some growth.\nMcKee once again draws a favorable matchup if you are playing that card before accounting for who the Eagles may still be without on Sunday night.\nWashington is 30th in passing points allowed (19.6 per game).\nWashington has lived and died by the blitz this season.\nThey are blitzing at a 42.5% rate, second in the league.\nWhen they blitz, Washington is allowing a 60.2% completion rate (13th) and 6.1 Y/A (12th).\nWithout it, they have allowed a 70.6% completion rate (29th) and 8.3 Y/A (30th).\nWhen blitzed over the past two weeks, McKee has completed 10 of 21 passes (47.6%) for 4.8 yards per attempt.\nEagles vs. Commanders Fantasy Running Back Preview\nSaquon Barkley\nBarkley suited up Monday night and turned 9 touches into 71 yards and a touchdown on only 10 snaps.\nHe was still dealing with his hamstring injury.\nOn the first run of the game, Barkley went for 29 yards but pulled up out of bounds instead of hitting another gear.\nHe returned to the game and scored his first touchdown of the year, but after the first quarter, he was limited to just 4 more touches on 8.3% of snaps.\nBarkley said he tweaked his hamstring on his first run of the game and then picked up a back injury trying to play through it.\nHe said neither was an issue and that his hamstring \"came out great\" despite what you have read.\nThe Eagles have a short week, so we will have to follow Barkley's availability in practice.\nIf he logs a full practice, we can feel more confident about going back to him, but the risk of re-injury still feels fragile and makes him a volatile RB2.\nThe thing is, Barkley has looked good on the small sample we have seen this year.\nEspecially in the context of this offense.\nHe is averaging 5.3 yards per touch.\nHe has a 44.2% success rate as a runner (12th among backs with 25-plus attempts) and a 10-plus-yard run on 14% of his carries (10th).\n50% of his runs have gained more yards than expected per Next Gen Stats, third in the league.\nBut he has finished just two games.\nBarkley rushed 15 times for 83 yards when these teams played in Week 1.\nA 42-yard scamper in the second quarter anchored his rushing day.\nWashington has allowed 3.8 YPC (10th) to running backs, allowing 0.89 yards before contact per rush (8th) on those runs.\nThey are third in the league in rate of runs allowed for 10 or more yards (5.7%).\nIf Barkley returns to sitting out, Dameon Pierce was placed on injured reserve on Wednesday, leaving Elijah Mitchell (6 touches for 34 yards) and Jaydon Blue as the backfield duo. \nRachaad White\nWhite led the backfield in Week 6, playing 70.5% of snaps and turning 11 touches into 57 yards and a touchdown.\nAs a rusher, White was ineffective, rushing 7 times for 27 yards.\nBut he saved his night by catching 4 of 5 targets for 30 yards and a touchdown out of the backfield.\nWhite had 57.9% of the backfield touches and 53.9% of the rushing attempts, which is a flimsy profile for an RB2/FLEX.\nThe door is still open for White to concede more rushing work here.\nJeremy McNichols had 5 touches for 13 yards while Austin Ekeler had 3 touches for 7 yards.\nThe Eagles are coming off an embarrassing showing on Monday night, allowing 196 rushing yards to the Dallas backfield, 155 of which came from Javonte Williams .\nThe week before that, they allowed 120 yards on the ground to Chuba Hubbard .\nIn the Week 1 matchup, Washington backs rushed 27 times for 101 yards (3.7 YPC) and a touchdown, led by Jacory Croskey-Merritt (16 rushes for 63 yards).\nEagles vs. Commanders Fantasy Wide Receiver Preview\nStefon Diggs\nDiggs caught 6 of 7 targets for 44 yards and his 4th touchdown of the year back in Week 6 before the bye.\nDan Quinn said the plan was to keep Diggs at a similar route rate, even after the Terry McLaurin injury.\nDiggs has run a route on 69.2% of the dropbacks in his games played.\nWhen McLaurin missed Week 4, Diggs was at 75.6%.\nDiggs had a 25% target share in that game, which was played by QB3 Athan Kaliakmanis .\nThat ballpark will still work for Diggs as a WR3 with scoring upside.\nDiggs leads Washington with a 27.3% target share.\nHe has 23.5% of red-zone targets in his games played, with a team-high 3 end-zone targets.\nDiggs has played 63.2% of his snaps in the slot, which provides matchup appeal here.\nThe Eagles have allowed the most fantasy points to slot receivers (18.3 per game).\nThey allow the most receptions to slot receivers (5.7 per game), with 8.9 yards per target (18th) and a 10.2% touchdown rate (28th).\nWashington wore out Cooper DeJean in the first matchup.\nDeJean allowed 7 of 9 targets in his coverage to be completed for 83 yards and a touchdown.\nDiggs caught 4 of 9 targets for 55 yards and a touchdown in the first matchup.\nAntonio Williams\nWilliams will take on a larger role after the bye with Terry McLaurin done for the season.\nWilliams has the highest route rate on the team with McLaurin off the field (70.8%).\nWhen McLaurin missed Week 4, Williams ran a route on 73.2% of the dropbacks, second only to Diggs.\nIn that game, Williams had a season-high 22.2% target share.\nHe caught only 3 of 8 targets for 21 yards in that game, but he was playing with Kaliakmanis.\nWilliams also had an 87-yard touchdown called back in that game.\nThe new opportunity puts Williams in play as a WR4/FLEX , but we still don't know if there's much ceiling to latch onto.\nWilliams is playing 42% of his snaps in the slot without McLaurin on the field, giving him a piece of the matchup notes laid out above.\nWilliams had a season-high 64 yards and his touchdown this season on only 11 routes in Week 1 when these teams played.\nMakai Lemon\nLemon had the best game of his early career on Monday night, catching 7 of 8 targets for 67 yards and his 1st NFL touchdown.\nWithout DeVonta Smith and Dontayvion Wicks , Lemon set season highs in route participation (92.1%) and targets share (24.2%).\nWe don't expect Smith back this week, and Wicks will impact Darius Cooper more than Lemon, so Lemon still has appeal as a WR4 to lead the receiving corps this week .\nHe only had 40 air yards on his targets and played 71% of his snaps from the slot, so he is still tied to a volume-necessity role.\nWashington has been better against slot receivers than outside, allowing 6.6 yards per target (8th) to slot receivers compared to 9.6 yards per target out wide (26th).\nDarius Cooper\nCooper started on Monday night and matched Lemon with a 92.1% route rate.\nBut he only caught 1 of 3 targets for 17 yards.\nIf Wicks remains out here, Cooper has another good matchup as WR4/FLEX or for single-game DFS.\nWashington is 30th in points allowed to outside receivers (26.7 per game).\nTreylon Burks\nBurks will also get more playing time with McLaurin out.\nWith McLaurin out in Week 4, Burks ran a route on 61% of the dropbacks, catching a 47-yard touchdown on his lone reception.\nBurks has played 75% of his snaps out wide, so he will face the worst of the matchup and line up most often against Quinyon Mitchell and Riq Woolen, making him a touchdown-or-bust play for single-game DFS.\nEagles vs. Commanders Fantasy Tight End Preview\nDallas Goedert\nGoedert caught 5 of 7 targets for 48 yards on Monday night.\nIn two games with McKee, Goedert has seen 8 and 7 targets, with targets on 21.6% and 29.2% of his routes.\nThis would be a smash spot if not for McKee, but Goedert still has matchup appeal and has shown a good floor with McKee to be an upside TE1.\nWashington is 31st in points allowed per target to tight ends (2.63), allowing 9.0 yards per target (29th) and a league-high 16.3% touchdown rate to the position.\nGoedert caught 4 of 5 targets for 77 yards and 2 touchdowns against Washington in Week 1.\nChig Okonkwo\nBefore the bye, Okonkwo played full-time for the first time this season in Week 6, catching all 7 of his targets for 69 yards.\nOkonkwo ran a route on a season-high 88.9% of the dropbacks.\nMcLaurin obviously left the game with an injury, but we had already seen McLaurin miss Week 4, and in that game Okonkwo ran a route on just 61% of the dropbacks.\nThat could be happenstance, but Washington may view him as one of their best offensive players without McLaurin moving forward.\nOkonkwo has target shares of 26.1% and 20% in his past two games, working as a fringe TE1/high-end TE2.\nThat role has more upside, but he faces another tough assignment here.\nThe Eagles aren't as dominant against tight ends this year as in previous seasons, but tight ends still account for only 14.7% of the fantasy points Philadelphia has allowed this season (4th).\nThe only high-end spike week they have allowed was Tommy Tremble , who had 2 touchdowns, the only 2 they have allowed to the position.\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" Panthers @ Packers \",\"Thursday Night Football\"],[\" Ravens @ Bills \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Vikings @ Lions \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Falcons @ Buccaneers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Colts @ Jaguars \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Titans @ Bengals \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Cardinals @ Cowboys \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Raiders @ Jets \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Browns @ Steelers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chargers @ Rams \",\"Sunday -- 4:05 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chiefs @ Broncos \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Patriots @ Dolphins \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Eagles @ Commanders \",\"Sunday Night Football\"],[\" Bears @ Seahawks \",\"Monday Night Football\"]],[{\"row\":0,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":0,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":1,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcellstyle\":{\"font-weight\":\"bold\"}},{\"row\":1,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":2,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":2,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcellstyle\":{\"font-weight\":\"bold\"}},{\"row\":2,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":2,