overs this season, 28th in the league.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nPatriots vs. Dolphins Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nDrake Maye (TRUST)\nMaye is on a roll, ripping off four straight weeks as the QB1, QB1, QB3, and QB2 in weekly scoring.\nHe has thrown 12 touchdowns to 2 interceptions in the past four weeks, completing 67.2% of his passes (10th) for a league-high 8.9 yards per pass attempt and a league-high 9% touchdown rate.\nHe has gotten the deep ball back, averaging 9.5 air yards per attempt over that span, second in the league.\nOn throws 10 or more yards downfield over that stretch, Maye has completed 31 of 48 passes (64.6%), third in the league, with a league-high 8 touchdowns.\nOn throws 10 or more yards downfield, Miami has allowed a league-high 66.7% completion rate (30 of 45) for a league-high 15.8 yards per pass attempt.\n77.8% (14 of 18) of the touchdowns against Miami have come through the air, the highest rate in the league.\nThe only quarterback who did not throw multiple touchdown passes against Miami was Kyler Murray .\nThe Dolphins are 31st in passing points allowed per attempt (0.61).\nOffenses against them have thrown 3.0% more than expected (7th), and they have allowed a higher pass rate than expected in five straight games.\nThat is still only an expected 52.7% dropback rate, but Miami has put up a fight for three straight games and forced passers to throw 37, 32, and 30 times over the span while still allowing efficiency per attempt.\nMiami has often put up a fight against New England at home, winning five of the past six home games, but Maye won his only start there a year ago in a 33-27 game.\nMalik Willis\nWillis is coming off his best game of the season on Sunday, closing the week as the QB4 (26 points).\nAs a passer, Willis completed a season-high 66.7% (14 of 21) of his passes for 208 yards, throwing for a season-high 9.9 yards per pass attempt and 2 touchdowns.\nIt was his first multi-touchdown passing game this season.\nHe also ran 8 times for 37 yards and a score.\nWillis has finally strung together some rushing consistency, raising his floor.\nHe has rushed for 5.6, 1.1, 13.4, and 9.7 fantasy points over the past four games, with the one down week in that stretch coming against the Vikings.\nOutside of the Minnesota game, he has 9, 8, and 8 rushing attempts over that span.\nWillis is still a volatile passer.\nLast week was his first game with over 6.0 Y/A since Week 2, and it was his first QB1 scoring week of the year, so we will keep things in perspective with him as a floor-based QB2 who can get over when the rushing and passing efficiency come together.\nThat said, Miami has played competent football for most of the past four weeks.\nThey have over 300 yards of offense in three of their past four games, with the exception being the Minnesota game.\nThis is still a matchup to keep expectations in check.\nNew England can rush the passer, ranking seventh in pressure rate (39.1%).\nThe only team Willis has faced that's been better at rushing the passer this season is the Vikings.\nWhen Willis has been pressured, he has thrown for 5.1 yards per pass attempt (23rd) with a 5.4% interception rate (29th) and a 21.1% sack rate (24th).\nThe Patriots will give up some explosive plays, though, which Willis has lived on.\nThe Patriots are allowing 11.2 yards per completion (19th) and a 47.6% completion rate on throws 20 or more yards downfield (22nd).\nPatriots vs. Dolphins Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdown