l\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nKansas City Rank @ Denver Rank\n-1.5 Spread 1.5\n21.5 Implied Total 20.0\n23.8 14 Points/Gm 20.6 24\n17.2 2 Points All./Gm 20.1 7\n59.8 25 Plays/Gm 57.1 30\n64.5 24 Opp. Plays/Gm 69.9 31\n6.4 2 Off. Yards/Play 4.7 26\n5 10 Def. Yards/Play 5 9\n44.9% 13 Rush% 43.3% 19\n55.2% 20 Pass% 56.8% 14\n43.2% 17 Opp. Rush % 41.7% 14\n56.9% 16 Opp. Pass % 58.3% 19\nThe Chiefs have converted 32.4% (11 of 34) of third downs over the past four weeks (28th), after converting 45.9% in the first three weeks (9th).\nThe Broncos have converted 31.1% of their third downs for the season, 31st in the league.\nThe Broncos average 5.1 plays per possession, the fewest in the league.\n13.5% of Denver's offensive plays have gained 10 or more yards, the lowest rate in the league.\nThe Chiefs have allowed a 26.7% touchdown rate on red-zone possessions (4 of 15), 2nd in the league.\nKansas City has allowed a touchdown on 14.8% of opponent possessions, 3rd in the league.\nDenver has allowed a touchdown on 17.5% of opponent possessions, 6th.\nThe Chiefs allow 2.0 sacks plus turnovers per game, the fewest in the league.\nThe Broncos allow 2.1 sacks plus turnovers per game, 2nd in the league.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nChiefs vs. Broncos Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nPatrick Mahomes\nMahomes is coming off a tough matchup in Seattle, completing 19 of 33 passes (57.6%) for a season-low 165 yards (5.0 Y/A) with no touchdowns and an interception.\nHe rushed two times for 14 yards.\nThe Chiefs never even got to the red zone on Sunday night, the first time ever in a game with Mahomes.\nHis 5.0 yards per pass attempt were the fifth-lowest of his career.\nThe Seahawks were not only a tough draw, but they also took away Kenneth Walker , exposing this offense's lack of top-down talent.\nWhile struggling in Seattle was hardly unexpected, Mahomes has closed his past four games as a QB2 scorer.\nThings do not objectively look a lot better here to move Mahomes out of that QB2 range .\nThe Chiefs have a 21.5-point team total on the road in Denver.\nMahomes had a 36.1% success rate on Sunday night, the same rate he had against Denver in Week 1.\nMahomes finished Week 1 as the QB9 (21.7 points), but he added 8.3 rushing points.\nHe has 4.8 rushing points since.\nMahomes produced only 13.4 passing points (QB17) in that game.\nHe threw a pair of touchdowns, but Mahomes completed only 55.6% (28th) of his passes for 6.8 yards per attempt (20th).\nMahomes was 28th in completion rate in Week 1 despite averaging only 4.1 air yards per attempt.\nHe completed 12.3% of his passes below expectations in Week 1.\nOn his completed passes, Mahomes averaged 1.1 air yards.\nHe was 0-of-4 on throws 10 or more yards downfield.\nThings should be better by default here, but the Denver defense is still a challenge.\nThey are allowing a 56.4% completion rate (2nd), 6.3 yards per pass attempt (6th), and a 3% touchdown rate (6th).\nIn seven full games in Denver, Mahomes has averaged 14.6 fantasy points per game, throwing 1 or fewer touchdown passes in five of those games.\nDenver also got Jonathon Cooper back on Sunday, and they could be getting Patrick Surtain and Talanoa Hufanga back this week.\nHowever, they could be without Zach Allen , who suffered a hamstring injury on Sunday.\nBo Nix\nGamers who rolled the dice on Nix based on the matchup last week were rewarded with a QB3 scoring week (26.2 points).\nWhile nobody will give back the fantasy points, the outcome was fortunate.\nNix largely made his day on two plays.\nThe first was a 62-yard touchdown run where he stepped up to scramble and just had green grass in front of him, going into the end zone untouched until he got to the goal line.\nBO NIX 62-YARD TOUCHDOWN RUN\nDENvsAZ on CBS/Paramount+\nStream on @NFLPlus pic.twitter.com/sxC4uWF33r\n- NFL (@NFL) October 25, 2026\nNix's other touchdown was a 52-yard screen pass to RJ Harvey .\nOverall, Nix still underwhelmed as a passer.\nHe completed a season-low 51.7% of his passes for 222 yards.\nTo their credit, Denver did try to go downfield more on Sunday.\nNix averaged a season-high 9.4 air yards per attempt.\nHe threw a season-high 50% of his passes 10 or more yards downfield and 33.3% 20 or more yards downfield.\nHe completed 4 of 12 on those 10-plus-yard throws and 1 of 8 on his 20-plus-yard throws.\nWhile Denver should build on that approach versus what we have seen from the passing game to open the year, this is a tough assignment, keeping Nix as a back-end QB2 .\nThe Chiefs have allowed 10.9 passing points per game (3rd), a 62.3% completion rate (9th), 6.5 yards per pass attempt (9th), and a 2.4% touchdown rate (2nd).\nNix had a nightmare outing in Week 1 in Kansas City.\nHe was the QB31 (5.4 points).\nHe averaged 4.7 yards per pass attempt.\nNix completed 9.1% of his passes below expectations (30th).\nNix was pressured on 51.5% of his dropbacks, 30th in the league.\nUnder pressure, he was 4 for 13 (30.8%) for 11 yards (0.8 Y/A) with an interception and 4 sacks.\nThe Broncos may also miss Jaylen Waddle this week, who aggravated a chest injury on an end-around play call late in the game while the game was in control.\nChiefs vs. Broncos Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" 