12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nLA Chargers Rank @ LA Rams Rank\n6.5 Spread -6.5\n19.0 Implied Total 25.5\n15.0 32 Points/Gm 24.3 11\n25.2 22 Points All./Gm 22.7 16\n61.8 16 Plays/Gm 68.3 3\n61.2 14 Opp. Plays/Gm 61.1 13\n4.6 28 Off. Yards/Play 5.8 7\n5.4 18 Def. Yards/Play 4.9 6\n45.6% 12 Rush% 38.9% 22\n54.5% 21 Pass% 61.1% 11\n50.4% 32 Opp. Rush % 44.9% 21\n49.6% 1 Opp. Pass % 55.1% 12\nThe Chargers are 0-5-1 against the spread this season, the worst in the league.\nThe Chargers are last in the NFL in offensive EPA (-72.3).\nThe Rams have scored a touchdown on 28.6% of their drives, 7th in the league.\nThe Chargers have scored a touchdown on a league-low 12.7% of their possessions.\nThe Chargers are one of two teams (Tennessee being the other) that have not led at the half in any game this season.\nThe Chargers have 12 pass plays of 20-plus yards, ahead of only the Titans (11) and Eagles (10).\n31.9% of the passing plays against the Chargers have gained 10 or more yards, the highest rate in the league.\nThe Rams have scored 6 points off takeaways this season, the fewest in the league.\nThe Chargers have allowed 47 points off turnovers, tied for the 2nd-most in the league.\nThe Chargers are allowing a league-high 6.0 sacks plus turnovers per game.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nChargers vs. Rams Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nMatthew Stafford\nAdjusting the offense around multiple injuries and controlling the game, the Rams had their lowest dropback rate of the season on Sunday (44.1%).\nStafford threw for only 195 yards on Sunday, his fewest since Week 1.\nHe has thrown for fewer than 7.0 yards per pass attempt in three of his past four games.\nThe Rams went run-heavy on Sunday, but they've adjusted their passing attack over the past few weeks, moving toward a more condensed approach.\nStafford's three lowest depth-of-throw rates have come over the past three weeks.\nHe has thrown 20.8%, 23.3%, and 24.1% of his passes at or behind the line of scrimmage after a 10.7% rate of those throws in Weeks 1 through 4.\nHe has thrown the ball 10 or more yards downfield on 23.3% and 20.7% of his passes in the past two games, his lowest rates of the year.\nThe Rams have thrown fewer passes to wide receivers because of injuries.\nThey have thrown 43.8% and 41.4% of their passes to wide receivers in the past two games, while tight ends have received 31.3% and 48.3% of targets.\nThat has raised Stafford's completion rates to 74.1%, 75.8%, and 79.3% in the past three weeks.\nHis yardage has dropped, but Stafford has made up ground with multiple touchdown passes in all three games, throwing 8 touchdowns to 1 interception.\nEven running hot in the touchdown department, Stafford has closed the past two weeks as the QB12 in weekly scoring.\nThis approach makes it hard for him to have front-end ceiling output.\nAs expected, the Rams leaned on 13 personnel for a season-high 50% of snaps on Sunday after Davante Adams ' injury.\nThey played 11 personnel on a season-low 35.3% of the snaps.\nWe should expect things to stay on a similar path.\nThe Chargers play man coverage only 10.6% of their snaps (29th) and two-high coverage 57.1% of the time (8th).\nAs a byproduct, they face the league's lowest depth of throw (5.4 air yards).\nThey are also a bad team, so teams not only throw short against them, but they are hardly throwing at all.\nThe Chargers face the league's highest run rate and only 28.5 pass attempts per game (2nd fewest).\nThat means Stafford will have to build the plane out of touchdown efficiency as a fringe QB1.\nThe Chargers have allowed just one quarterback to finish higher than QB15 versus them this season, and that came with 3 touchdown passes.\nJustin Herbert\nHerbert returns from his bye after the worst stretch of his career.\nHerbert has completed 59.4% of his passes (28th) for 6.3 yards per pass attempt (25th), a 2.8% touchdown rate (29th), a 4.4% interception rate (31st), and a 10.4% sack rate (31st).\nHe ranks 31st in passing points per dropback (0.21) and hasn't had a week higher than QB22 all season.\nThe Chargers should be healthier out of the bye with the potential to get Ladd McConkey , Joe Alt , and Kayode Awosika back.\nWe'll also learn what (if anything) this offensive coaching staff has adjusted during the week off.\nThere have been swirling rumors that Mike McDaniel is on the immediate hot seat, as someone will eventually take the first fall for this season's failure.\nUntil we see proof the concepts are working, there is no reason to be bullish on using Herbert as more than a back-end QB2.\nThe Rams are also not a good matchup.\nThey have allowed a 57.8% completion rate (3rd) and 5.9 yards per pass attempt (2nd).\nThey have allowed a 4.4% touchdown rate (18th) if you are adventurous.\nChargers vs. Rams Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" Panthers @ Packers \",\"Thursday Night Football\"],[\" Ravens @ Bills \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Vikings @ Lions \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Falcons @ Buccaneers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Colts @ Jaguars \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Titans @ Bengals \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Cardinals @ Cowboys \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Raiders @ Jets \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Browns @ Steelers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Charger