% 28\n57.6% 13 Pass% 63.1% 5\n47.7% 28 Opp. Rush % 40.1% 9\n52.3% 5 Opp. Pass % 60.0% 24\nThe Bengals have led by double digits for 23.7% of their offensive snaps, 3rd in the league.\nTennessee has trailed by double digits for 35% of their offensive snaps, 31st in the league.\nThe Bengals have a +11 sack differential, 2nd in the league.\nCincinnati has scored 48 points off takeaways, 2nd in the league.\nTennessee has been outscored 99-37 in the first half (-62), the worst margin in the league.\nThe Titans are one of two teams (the Chargers being the other) that haven't led at the half in any game this season.\nThe Bengals have converted a league-high 50.6% of third downs.\nCincinnati has allowed a 48.1% conversion rate defensively on third down (30th).\nThe Titans have converted 35.4% of third downs, 27th.\nOnly 20.8% of the drives against Tennessee result in a three-and-out, the lowest rate in the league.\n83.3% (15 of 18) of Cincinnati's touchdowns have come via passing, the 3rd-highest rate.\n60% (9 of 15) of the touchdowns against Tennessee have come via rushing, the 2nd-highest rate.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nTitans vs. Bengals Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nJoe Burrow\nBurrow started slowly on Sunday.\nIn the first half, the Bengals had zero points, with Burrow going 6 of 12 for 43 yards (3.6 Y/A) (2.9 fantasy points).\nHe then turned it on in the second half, going 16 of 18 for 249 yards (13.8 Y/A) with 4 touchdowns (25.9 points) to finish with a QB1 overall scoring week.\nBurrow has four straight games inside the top-10, reeling off 20-plus points in each.\nThis is another spot for Burrow to stay efficient as a QB1.\nThe Titans have allowed a 69.2% completion rate (31st) and 7.7 yards per pass attempt (26th).\nBut they have saved face by only allowing a 2.7% touchdown rate (3rd).\nIf they keep allowing that much efficiency, passing touchdowns will start coming, and Burrow is a good bet to kick that door down.\nHe has completed 73.3% of his passes (6th) with a league-high 13.3% touchdown rate.\nThe Titans are a run funnel because they are so bad.\nTeams have thrown the ball at a 55.8% rate against them, compared with a 58% expected rate.\nBut that is not the Bengals game.\nCincinnati leads the league in pass rate over expectation and has thrown the ball over expectation in every game this season.\nThey have a 65.7% dropback rate on a 57.3% expected rate.\nCam Ward\nWard has thrown for more than 200 yards in one game this season.\nHe has not thrown multiple touchdown passes in a game this year.\nWard is 30th in passing points per dropback (0.23), 32nd in yards per pass attempt (5.7), and 32nd in touchdown rate (1.9%).\nIf you have to lean on Ward in 2QB formats due to byes or injuries, the Bengals have allowed viable QB2 games to Malik Willis (18.9 points) and Tyler Huntley (15.1 points) in the past two games .\nThe Bengals rank 30th in rushing yards allowed to quarterbacks (25.8 per game), which is the angle to play up for Ward to tack on some cheap points.\nTitans vs. Bengals Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" Panthers @ Packers \",\"Thursday Night Football\"],[\" Ravens @ Bills \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Vikings @ Lions \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Falcons @ Buccaneers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Colts @ Jaguars \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Titans @ Bengals \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Cardinals @ Cowboys \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Raiders 