:3,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":10,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":11,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":11,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":11,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":11,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":11,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":12,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":12,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nBaltimore Rank @ Buffalo Rank\n3.5 Spread -3.5\n25.0 Implied Total 28.5\n24.1 12 Points/Gm 32.8 1\n23.9 18 Points All./Gm 28.5 30\n61.7 17 Plays/Gm 59.8 25\n60.1 11 Opp. Plays/Gm 68.5 30\n5.9 6 Off. Yards/Play 6.5 1\n5.3 13 Def. Yards/Play 5.6 24\n49.1% 3 Rush% 49.0% 4\n50.9% 30 Pass% 51.0% 29\n42.3% 16 Opp. Rush % 38.4% 5\n57.7% 17 Opp. Pass % 61.6% 28\nGames involving Buffalo average a league-high 61.3 combined points per game.\nThe Bills lead the NFL in points per drive on offense (3.13).\nBuffalo allows the most points per drive on defense (2.76).\nBuffalo leads the league in runs of 10 or more yards per game (5.2).\nBaltimore is second with 4.1 runs of 10 or more yards per game.\nBuffalo leads the league in plays of 15-plus yards (8.8), 20-plus yards (5.3), and 30-plus yards (2.3) per game.\n22.9% of the plays against Buffalo have gained 10 or more yards, the 3rd-highest rate in the league.\nThe Ravens have 25 more penalties than their opponents, the worst margin in the league.\nBaltimore has outscored opponents 118-53 (+65) in the first half this season, the league's best margin.\nThe Ravens have been outscored 114-51 (-63) in the second half this season, the league's worst margin.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nRavens vs. Bills Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nJosh Allen (TRUST)\nAllen comes out of the bye leading all quarterbacks in fantasy points per game (26.9).\nHe has been a top-10 scorer in five of six games this season.\nBuffalo has made good on a few offseason promises.\nAllen is playing under center at a career-high 55.2% rate, which has led to a career-high 35.7% play-action rate.\nAllen is third in the league in fantasy points on play-action passing, completing 40 of 61 (65.6%) for 9.0 yards per pass attempt (3rd).\nAfter a career-low 7.3 air yards per pass attempt last season, Allen is pushing the ball downfield more, averaging 8.3 air yards per throw (7th).\nThat has led to career-high marks in yards per pass attempt (8.8) and yards per completion (13.2).\nAllen has cooled off downfield lately.\nOver his past three games, Allen has completed 12 of 29 (41.4%) throws 10 or more yards downfield, 26th in the league over that span.\nBut he is expected to get D.J. Moore back this week.\nWith Moore on the field this season, Allen has completed 65.5% (19 of 29) of his passes for 10+ yards, which would rank second in the league over the full season.\nAllen has only 72 dropbacks with Moore on the field this season, but on those snaps, he has completed 67.7% of his passes for 9.6 yards per attempt.\nOn 128 dropbacks without Moore on the field, Allen has completed 66% of his passes for 8.3 yards per pass attempt.\nIt has also been a benefit to gamers that Buffalo's defense is as bad as Allen has had, which has pushed Buffalo.\nThe Bills have already played in five games in which they have allowed 24 or more points.\nThat is one short of the most the Bills have allowed in a season since 2020.\nBuffalo has already allowed 30 or more points in four games this season.\nThe most they have allowed in a season since Allen's breakout in 2020 is five.\nThey have allowed 29-plus points in five of six games, with the only exception being the Chargers game.\nSince 2020, when Allen's defense allows 30-plus points, he averages 30.4 fantasy points.\nWhen they allow 24 or more points (below the Baltimore team total), Allen averages 28.1 fantasy points per game.\nYou already know how we handle these types of big-game environments.\nFor now, we assume Lamar Jackson is drawing live to return this week.\nIf so, we expect the high-scoring atmosphere implied here.\nEveryone remembers the 41-40 shootout these teams played to kick off last season.\nIn that game, Allen had 38.8 fantasy points.\nThe Ravens have had a light schedule so far, facing Daniel Jones , Tyler Shough , Cam Ward , Michael Penix , and Deshaun Watson .\nThe other games were against Dak Prescott and Joe Burrow .\nThey held Prescott to 6.9 yards per pass attempt, but it was also a 34-31 game in which Prescott still got 18.9 fantasy points despite throwing only 1 touchdown.\nThey just allowed Burrow to be the QB1 overall last week (28.9 points).\nThe Ravens have allowed rushing production to a handful of guys who are not in Allen's weight class.\nTyler Shough (23 yards and a touchdown), Dak Prescott (19 yards), Cam Ward (27 yards), Deshaun Watson (29 yards), and Burrow (12 yards) all added double-digit rushing yardage against Baltimore.\nAllen has never faced Jesse Minter , but he has thrown multiple touchdowns in three of four games against Miami's defenses led by Anthony Weaver .\nLamar Jackson\nWe are starting off another week under the premise that this could be the one we see Jackson return to the field.\nLast week felt like the closest Jackson has come to getting back, but ultimately he was unable to suit up Sunday.\nWe will monitor his status and adjust as needed throughout the week.\nIf Jackson is ready to play, he moves back into the front-end QB1 field .\nJackson has been hyper-efficient this season, completing 70.8% of his passes (2nd), averaging 10.1 yards per pass attempt, 14.2 yards per completion, and a 6.3% touchdown rate (7th).\nJackson averages 0.53 passing points per dropback, which would lead the league over the full season.\nWith Zay Flowers on the field, Jackson is at 0.63 passing points per dropback.\nThe hope is that Jackson's potential return and the matchup push Baltimore to be more aggressive, since they have had issues running the ball behind their patchwork offensive line.\nWhen we last saw Jackson in the first half of Week 4, that was happening.\nThe Ravens were 8% above pass-rate expectations in the first half of that game, and the offense thrived.\nThe Bills have allowed 21.3 passing points per game, 31st in the league.\nThey rank 27th in passing points allowed per attempt (0.53).\nEvery quarterback they've faced, except Justin Herbert , has thrown multiple touchdowns.\nTo be fair to Buffalo, they have faced a tough passing schedule, but that continues here if Jackson starts.\nWhen Jackson works from a clean pocket, he completes 78.8% of his passes (4th) for a league-high 11.5 yards per pass attempt.\nEven when pressured, Jackson has strong splits relative to his peers, completing 53.3% of his passes (4th) for 7.0 Y/A (9th).\nRunning the ball is where Jackson's ankle is a wild card.\nLast year, when he was injured and returned, he didn't run late in the year.\nWe want him moving here to tap into that extra gear for fantasy.\nThe Bills have faced only one mobile quarterback, Drake Maye , who rushed for 54 yards.\nRavens vs. Bills Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8