1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nCleveland Rank @ Pittsburgh Rank\n3.5 Spread -3.5\n18.0 Implied Total 21.5\n19.0 28 Points/Gm 21.9 22\n20.9 12 Points All./Gm 19.7 5\n60.0 24 Plays/Gm 63.9 11\n63.0 17 Opp. Plays/Gm 61.0 12\n5.0 23 Off. Yards/Play 5.1 22\n5 11 Def. Yards/Play 5 7\n43.8% 15 Rush% 35.8% 31\n56.2% 18 Pass% 64.2% 2\n41.7% 15 Opp. Rush % 39.8% 8\n58.3% 18 Opp. Pass % 60.2% 25\nThe Browns are 2-26 on the road in Pittsburgh since 2000, including 22 consecutive regular-season losses.\nThe Browns have led for 20.5% of their offensive plays, 26th in the league and the lowest rate among teams with a winning record.\nThe Browns have been outscored 58-13 in the first quarter this season, the league's worst margin.\nCleveland allows 4.9 sacks plus turnovers per game, 31st in the league.\nThe Steelers are allowing 1.75 points per drive, 4th in the league.\nThe Browns are allowing 1.87 points per drive, 8th.\nCleveland has a 63% success rate against the pass (3rd) compared to 58.7% against the run (23rd).\n20.9% of Pittsburgh's passing plays have gained 10 or more yards, 29th in the league.\n92.9% (13 of 14) of Pittsburgh's touchdowns have come through the air, the highest rate in the league.\nThe Steelers have thrown the ball on a league-high 68.6% of red-zone plays.\nPittsburgh has converted 47.4% (9 of 19) of its red-zone trips into touchdowns, 27th in the league.\nThe Steelers have the lowest success rate on passing plays targeting wide receivers (39.9%). The league average is 48.8%.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nBrowns vs. Steelers Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nAaron Rodgers\nRodgers is completing 61.6% of his passes (24th) for just 6.5 yards per attempt (23rd), but a 4.9% touchdown rate (12th) and 40.9 dropbacks per game (6th) keep him in play as a viable QB2 with three QB1 scoring weeks.\nOne of those was against Cleveland in Week 4.\nRodgers was the QB5 (22 points), completing 22 of 40 passes (55%) for 299 yards (7.5 Y/A) with 3 touchdowns, a two-point conversion, and 2 interceptions.\nRodgers scored 14.2 of his fantasy points in the fourth quarter that night chasing points.\nIn the first matchup, Rodgers was 13 of 26 (50%) for 145 yards (5.6 Y/A) with a touchdown and an interception heading into the final quarter, then had 154 yards (11.0 Y/A) with 2 touchdowns and an interception in the final quarter.\nThe Browns have kept games close, so that remains a possibility, but both teams have split home-field wins over the past five seasons, so chasing previous outcomes in season hasn't worked.\nAs noted above, the Steelers have dominated this rivalry at home.\nI prefer to use Rodgers as a QB2 in the rematch, not playing for the same jailbreak game script.\nThe Browns played man coverage on 71.1% of the snaps in the first matchup.\nRodgers sees the most man coverage in the league (40.4%), but averages only 6.0 Y/A (22nd) with a 51.8% completion rate (27th) against it.\nHe went 13 of 28 (46.4%) against man coverage in the first matchup but did average 7.6 Y/A with 2 touchdowns.\nThe Browns sacked him 5 times and pressured Rodgers on a season-high 42.2% rate.\nDeshaun Watson\nWatson is coming off his second QB1 scoring week of the season in Tennessee.\nHe did not throw a touchdown, but he ran one in while throwing for a season-high 308 yards.\nWatson has been at his best this season when the Browns rein him in, keep the ball short, and let their playmakers do the work after the catch.\nThey had 201 yards after the catch on Sunday, the second-most in the league.\n56.4% of Watson's passing yards this season have come after the catch, a career high.\nWatson has only thrown 29.7% of his passes 10 or more yards downfield (28th).\nWhen he has thrown those passes, he has completed 43.1% (25 of 58), 25th in the league.\nWatson is a QB2 here .\nHe was the QB21 (12.9 points) in the first matchup, completing 24 of 33 passes (72.7%) for 268 yards (8.1 Y/A) with a touchdown and an interception, rushing for 22 yards.\nThat was in a game where Jamel Dean left with an injury.\nPittsburgh has played sound pass defense overall, allowing 11.2 passing points per game (4th), 6.7 yards per pass attempt (14th), and a 2.9% touchdown rate (4th).\nBrowns vs. Steelers Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sharp's betting recommendations for 2026.\n- 2025 Record: 500-374 (57%) +70.77 Units -\n- 2025 Best Bet Record: 17-3 (85%) -\n- Computer Totals Record Last 5 Years: 144-65 (69%) -\nLEARN ABOUT ALL-ACCESS\nFantasy Packages : Unlocks all of Rich Hribar's industry-leading fantasy content for 2026.\nLEARN ABOUT FANTASY\nAlready a Subscriber? Log In\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" Panthers @ Packers \",\"Thursday Night Football\"],[\" Ravens @ Bills \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Vikings @ Lions \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Falcons @ Buccaneers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Colts @ Jaguars \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Titans @ Bengals \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Cardinals @ Cowboys \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Raiders @ Jets \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Browns @ Steelers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chargers @ Rams \",\"Sunday -- 4:05 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chiefs @ Broncos \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Patriots @ Dolphins \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Eagles @ Commanders \",\"Sunday Night Football\"],[\" Bears @ Seahawks \",\"Monday Night Football\"]],[{\"row\":0,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":0,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":1,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcellstyle\":{\"font-weight\":\"bold\"}},{\"row\":1,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\"