visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":9,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":9,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":10,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":10,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":10,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":10,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":10,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":11,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":11,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":11,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":11,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":11,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":12,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":12,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nLas Vegas Rank @ NY Jets Rank\n1.5 Spread -1.5\n22.0 Implied Total 23.5\n26.1 7 Points/Gm 19.6 27\n28.1 29 Points All./Gm 21.4 13\n64.3 9 Plays/Gm 58.1 29\n62.9 16 Opp. Plays/Gm 64.1 22\n5.3 17 Off. Yards/Play 5.2 19\n5.9 30 Def. Yards/Play 4.9 5\n38.7% 24 Rush% 44.0% 14\n61.3% 9 Pass% 56.0% 19\n45.9% 25 Opp. Rush % 48.3% 29\n54.1% 8 Opp. Pass % 51.7% 4\nThe Raiders have scored 33.0 more points than their implied totals this season, the most in the league.\nThe Raiders have scored on 46.2% of their drives, 6th in the league.\nThe Raiders are allowing a league-high 3.25 points per drive on defense over the past four weeks.\nThe Raiders are allowing a league-high 6.5 yards per play on defense over the past four weeks.\nThe Jets have converted 45% (9 of 20) of their red-zone trips into touchdowns, 31st in the league.\nThe Raiders have converted 46.4% (13 of 28) of their red-zone trips into touchdowns, 29th.\nThe Raiders average 12.0 passing plays of 10-plus yards per game, 3rd in the league.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nRaiders vs. Jets Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nKirk Cousins\nCousins enters Week 8 leading the NFL in passing touchdowns (16), third in passing yards (1,877), and QB11 in fantasy points per game (18.8).\nThe Raiders have played a string of games in the past month against true title contenders, showing they are still a ways off.\nThose game environments helped Cousins accrue a ton of counting stats.\nCousins has the most fourth-quarter fantasy points over the past four weeks (38.3), including a game where he was benched.\nIn those fourth quarters, Cousins has averaged 9.3 yards per pass attempt with 5 touchdown passes.\nHe is the QB20 in points scored over the opening three quarters of those weeks (36.9), throwing for 6.5 Y/A and 2 touchdowns.\nThis is a spot where the Raiders could find themselves in a more neutral script.\nIn Weeks 1, 2, and 3, when they won all three games, Cousins dropped back to pass 33.3 times per game (21st in the league).\nOver the past four weeks, he has dropped back 42.3 times per game (5th).\nOpponents against the Jets have thrown the ball 2.2% below expectations (25th).\nWhile that lowers the floor for Cousins, he still hangs around as a high-end QB2 and potential streamer for those who have been using him.\nThe Raiders may not be able to run the ball effectively, so they need to pass to score.\nIn neutral game scripts, this offense can lean more into their base offense and utilize play action.\nCousins is still the top-rated passer using play action, completing 70.8% (46 of 65) of those passes for 8.5 Y/A with 8 touchdowns and zero interceptions.\nThe Jets also haven't been as strong defensively lately as they were at the start of the season.\nAllowing a QB3 week to Drake Maye in Week 6 is nothing to be ashamed of, but then coming back the next week and allowing 9.9 yards per pass attempt and 2 touchdowns to Malik Willis raises some eyebrows.\nThe Jets have allowed multiple passing touchdowns in four of their past six games.\nMy one worry about ceiling upside is that the Jets grind this game out without scoring truckloads of points like the Raiders have given up the past month.\nLas Vegas has not stopped the run, and the Jets will run if you let them.\nThe good news for the Jets is that Cousins isn't going to run.\nThe Jets have allowed Deshaun Watson (40 yards), Maye (24 yards), and Willis (37 yards and a touchdown) to extend plays against them.\nGeno Smith\nSmith took advantage of a good matchup and needed to score points last week to deliver a QB5 (23.5 points) scoring week against Miami.\nSmith completed 23 of 30 passes (76.7%) for 287 yards (9.6 Y/A) and 3 touchdowns, throwing an interception.\nSmith has completed 70% of his passes (3rd in the league) for 7.8 yards per attempt (8th), with 10 touchdowns and 2 interceptions.\nThe Jets haven't let Smith air it out, though, which is a concern.\nSmith is averaging 33.9 dropbacks per game (20th).\nPart of his reduced volume is because he's been efficient with his pass attempts, but the Jets are also 20th in pass rate versus expectations.\nSmith's two QB1 scoring weeks this season have come chasing points against the Lions and Dolphins.\nThat makes Smith more of a high-floor QB2, but he should be efficient when passing, with added upside that the game gets pushed into a better game state than implied.\nThe Jets just lost to Miami, so that is well within the range of outcomes.\nThe Raiders are allowing a 65.8% completion rate (20th), 7.7 yards per pass attempt (28th), and a 7.2% touchdown rate (30th).\nThey have allowed multiple passing touchdowns in five straight games.\nRaiders vs. Jets Fantasy Running Back Preview\nTo continue reading this article\nselect one of the packages below\nAll-Access : Unlocks all fantasy content & Warren Sh