ualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":8,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":8,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":8,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":8,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":9,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":9,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":9,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":9,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":9,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":9,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":10,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":10,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":10,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":10,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":10,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":10,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":11,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":11,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":11,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":11,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":11,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":11,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4},{\"row\":12,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":12,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":12,\"col\":2,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":2,\"prop\":2},{\"row\":12,\"col\":3,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":3,\"prop\":3},{\"row\":12,\"col\":4,\"visualRow\":12,\"visualCol\":4,\"prop\":4}],[],[]] []\nCarolina Rank @ Green Bay Rank\n3.5 Spread -3.5\n22.0 Implied Total 25.5\n26.5 6 Points/Gm 20.6 24\n27.7 27 Points All./Gm 20.7 11\n66.0 6 Plays/Gm 61.3 19\n64.8 25 Opp. Plays/Gm 66.3 27\n5.8 9 Off. Yards/Play 5.4 16\n5.4 17 Def. Yards/Play 4.9 4\n36.9% 29 Rush% 33.6% 32\n63.1% 4 Pass% 66.4% 1\n45.8% 24 Opp. Rush % 44.2% 19\n54.2% 9 Opp. Pass % 55.8% 14\nThe Panthers average the most penalty yards per game (79.2).\nThe Packers average the second-most penalty yards per game (78.0).\nThe Packers have scored 22.75 fewer points than their implied totals this season, 31st in the league.\nThe Panthers have scored 23.25 more points than their implied totals this season, 3rd in the league.\nGames involving the Panthers average a league-high 258.5 combined rushing yards per game.\nGames involving the Packers average a league-low 174.9 combined rushing yards per game.\nThe Panthers have a 60.7% success rate defending passing plays (5th), compared with 57.3% against rushing plays (30th).\nThe Packers have a 30.6% success rate on running back rushing plays, the worst rate in the league.\n81.8% of Green Bay's offensive yardage has come through the pass, the highest rate in the league.\nThe Packers average 4.3 passing plays of 20-plus yards per game, the most in the league.\n4.3% of the passing plays against Carolina have gained 20 or more yards, the 2nd-lowest rate in the league.\n5% of the passing plays against Green Bay have gained 20 or more yards, the 3rd-lowest rate in the league.\nGreen Bay has a takeaway on a league-high 16.9% of opponent possessions.\nTrust = spike production for that player\nPanthers vs. Packers Fantasy Quarterback Preview\nJordan Love\nLove completed 27 of 45 passes (60%) for 274 yards (6.1 Y/A), 2 touchdowns, and 1 interception Sunday in Detroit.\nLove has now completed over 60% of his passes in two games this season.\nHe has thrown for fewer than 7.0 yards per pass attempt in five of seven games.\nHe has thrown multiple passing touchdowns in all but one game.\n81.3% (13 of 16) of Green Bay's touchdowns have been passing, fourth in the league.\nThe good news is the Packers kept throwing the ball heavily on Sunday, even with Josh Jacobs back.\nGreen Bay threw 13% more than expected against the Lions.\nGreen Bay is third in pass rate over expectation for the season (+5.8%), with a pass over expectation in six of seven games.\nThat has kept Love in the fringe QB1 mix for fantasy despite his uneven passing efficiency.\nThe Panthers are a bit more of a run funnel for opposing offenses.\nTeams have thrown the ball 3.2% below expectations against Carolina, the fifth-lowest rate.\nThe Packers haven't been able to run the ball effectively, but this may be a chance to lean on Jacobs in his second game.\nWhen these teams met in Week 9 last season, the Packers threw 3.6% below expectations.\nHopefully, Matt LaFleur learned from that, as Green Bay lost 16-13 as 13-point favorites.\nIn that game, Love was QB24 (9.0 fantasy points), completing 26 of 37 passes (70.3%) for 273 yards (7.4 Y/A) with no touchdowns and an interception.\nLove also faced Ejiro Evero in 2023.\nIn that matchup, Love was 17 of 28 (60.7%) for 219 yards (7.8 Y/A) with 2 touchdowns and zero interceptions.\nThe Panthers rank second in disguising their coverage pre-snap (40.1%).\nLove has had some hiccups against disguised coverage this season, completing 53.6% of his passes for 6.5 Y/A with 0.36 fantasy points per dropback.\nAgainst static post-snap looks, Love has completed 62.6% of his passes for 7.6 Y/A and 0.48 fantasy points per dropback.\nIn that matchup last season, Love threw for 5.9 Y/A against disguised coverages compared to 8.3 Y/A against static looks.\nCarolina has held up against the pass this season, ranking ninth in passing points allowed per attempt (0.37), sixth in completion rate (60.5%), fifth in yards per pass attempt (6.3), and eighth in touchdown rate (3.5%).\nThey have allowed Caleb Williams (18.8 passing points) and Jared Goff (20.5) to turn good fantasy lines, so we don't have to completely hide.\nI am pricing in Love as a fringe QB1 option this week.\nI would prefer to use him as an upside QB2 if I had the luxury, but I would not go wild looking for a streamer to replace him.\nThere is some volatility here if Green Bay tries to run and Love stays on the low end of completion rate and yards per pass attempt.\nBut the Packers may have a broken rushing attack, and the Panthers also play aggressively, so we could get a game where both sides push each other.\nBryce Young\nSunday was a disastrous outing for Young and the Carolina offense.\nYoung completed 14 of 27 passes (51.9%) for 163 yards (6.0 Y/A) with 1 touchdown and 2 interceptions in a 43-13 home thumping against the Buccaneers.\nYoung did not even play in the fourth quarter of the blowout loss.\nThe yards per pass attempt were a season low.\nYoung also took a season-high 5 sacks.\nAnother down note for Young and this passing offense is that Taylor Moton was lost for the season after just coming back in Week 6.\nWith Moton off the field this season, Young has been pressured on a league-high 46.9% of his dropbacks.\nMonroe Freeling has played right tackle for Moton, allowing 16 pressures and an 8.7% pressure rate, second-highest on the team behind left tackle Rasheed Walker (10.1%).\nAs good as the season has started for Young, he has completed fewer than 55% of his passes in three of his past four starts.\nHe is 20th in completion rate versus expectation (-3.1%) over that span.\nHowever, as we covered with Love, the Panthers have taken a pass-heavy approach this season to offset the lack of extreme accuracy.\nCarolina is second in pass rate over expectations (+6.9%), throwing the ball more than expected in every game this season.\nGreen Bay allows 12.1 passing points per game (7th), 6.4 yards per pass attempt (8th), and a 3.7% touchdown rate (10th).\nThe Packers were not challenged by good passing games until the past two weeks.\nThey held up great against Dak Prescott in Week 6 (10.3 points) but then allowed 303 passing yards and 9.5 Y/A to Jared Goff last week (18 points).\nYoung has had a good track record against Jonathan Gannon .\nHe has faced him twice, scoring 27.1 and 21.3 fantasy points.\nYoung has thrown for only 6.0 Y/A and 6.1 Y/A in those outings but has thrown 5 touchdowns.\nGreen Bay has also run a high rate of disguised coverages this season, ranking third behind Carolina (39.5%).\nYoung has been better against those looks, throwing for 9.1 Y/A and a 65.6% completion rate, compared with 56.7% completion and 6.9 Y/A against static coverages post-snap.\nI am treating Young similarly in the fringe QB1 range.\nI also would prefer to use him as a QB2 to price in some downside , but I don't want to completely throw out the start of his season because of last week's dud.\nThis game still has the potential to feature a lot of passing from both sides.\nPanthers vs. Packers Fantasy Running Back Preview\nChuba Hubbard\nHubbard finished with 60 yards on 11 touches on Sunday, his fewest touches in a game this season.\nHubbard still played 78.6% of snaps and handled 11 of 15 backfield touches with the starters still playing.\nThe offense just flatlined overall.\nBefore Sunday, Hubbard had at least 20 touches and 100 total yards in each of his previous three games.\nHe was an RB1 scorer in every game before Sunday.\nHubbard is in a spot to rebound as a back-end RB1/high RB2 .\nGreen Bay has played a lot better against the run since getting shredded by Atlanta in Week 3.\nOutside of that game, they have allowed 3.3 YPC to running backs with 0.54 yards before contact per rush.\nLast week, they did everything they could against Jahmyr Gibbs and Detroit.\nThey did allow 121 rushing yards, but it took 30 attempts, while the Packers allowed only 0.67 yards before contact per rush.\nThe Packers have given up some receiving work to backs, ranking 19th in receiving points allowed to backfields (8.6 per game).\nJosh Jacobs\nJacobs handled 16 touches for 91 total yards and a touchdown in his first appearance this season.\nHe played 53.8% of snaps and handled 66.7% of backfield touches.\nJacobs did not cure the ailing run game, rushing 11 times for 36 yards (3.3 YPC).\nHe did make an impact as a pass catcher, however, catching 5 of 7 targets for 55 yards and a touchdown.\nJosh Jacobs gives the Packers the lead in the third quarter\nGBvsDET on FOX/FOX One\nStream on @NFLPlus pic.twitter.com/G752Vd6fFn\n- NFL (@NFL) October 25, 2026\nHe also dropped what would have been a second receiving touchdown on a 17-yard wheel route.\nOne thing underrated about Jacobs last year was his role as a pass catcher.\nBefore his injury last year, Jacobs saw a spike in receiving usage, running a route on 51.1% of dropbacks (RB13) with 3.1 receptions per game (RB7).\nThat gives him added outs if Green Bay cannot get the run game on track and still needs to stay pass-heavy, even with Jacobs back.\nThis game is a short turnaround from his first action, but we should expect Jacobs to ramp up his snap share and touches as we move on.\nThis matchup offers upside for Jacobs as a back-end RB1 .\nCarolina has been all over the place defending backs.\nThey have had a few strong showings this year against Bijan Robinson (81 total yards) and Jahmyr Gibbs (77 total yards) , but they've also allowed spike weeks to  D'Andre   Swift (134 yards and 3 touchdowns) and Bucky Irving (110 yards and 2 touchdowns).\nThey allowed 258 total yards and 4 touchdowns to the Chicago backs in Week 1, then 250 yards and 3 touchdowns to the Tampa Bay backfield on Sunday.\nAll things added up, they have allowed 5.5 yards per touch (31st) and are 30th in points allowed per touch (1.01) to backfields.\nPanthers vs. Packers Fantasy Wide Receiver Preview\nTetairoa McMillan\nMcMillan caught 4 of 6 targets for 40 yards in Sunday's beatdown.\nMcMillan still has solid WR2 upside , with 25.6% of Carolina's targets (WR13) and 2.22 yards per route run (WR17).\nThe Packers have been good against the pass overall this season, but they have been vulnerable to receiver production given the volume they have seen.\nThe Packers face 21.9 targets per game to wide receivers (3rd most).\nGreen Bay is 27th in points allowed to WR1 targets (18.4 per game).\nThey are 28th in points allowed to outside receivers (25.4 per game), where McMillan plays 75.7% of his snaps, allowing 8.1 yards per target (18th) and a 5% touchdown rate (22nd) to those receivers.\nChristian Watson\nWatson grabbed 5 of 8 targets for 57 yards on Sunday.\nWatson has been out-targeted by Golden, but he still has at least 8 targets in every game except one because Green Bay has remained pass-heavy.\nWatson still has 21.3% of the Green Bay targets (WR23), 31.4% of the air yards (WR24), and 2.48 yards per route run (WR10).\nWatson sees high-leverage targets, averaging 13.9 air yards per target (WR20) with 39.1% of the Green Bay targets into the end zone (WR9).\nThat is still the profile of an upside WR2, and we have upside here.\nCarolina ends up in single-high coverage 59.5% of the time, the fifth-highest rate in the league.\nWatson has been targeted on a team-high 24.3% of his routes against single-high coverages for 2.09 yards per route.\nAgainst Cover 3 (which Carolina runs at a league-high 51.7% rate), Watson has been targeted on a team-high 26.9% of his routes for 2.71 yards per route run.\nCarolina wasn't tested last week because the Bucs didn't have to throw, but they are still missing Jaycee Horn and Mike Jackson on the outside.\nGolden is also entering this week a bit banged up, which could push some work Watson's way.\nMatthew Golden\nGolden caught 6 of 12 targets for 39 yards in Detroit.\nGolden left some meat on the bone again.\nHe was the WR1 in expected points for Week 7 , but failed to cash in on the opportunities.\nThe results were hardly flattering, but Golden received double-digit targets for the fourth time this season.\nGolden has 26.2% of the Green Bay targets (WR8), 39.9% of the air yards (WR8), and 2.06 yards per route run (WR21).\nGolden was banged up in the second quarter on Sunday.\nHe played through shoulder and foot injuries, but a short week could affect him.\nFollow his status heading into Thursday night.\nBeyond his injury status, there are added signals that this could be a Watson week over Golden, but if Golden is good to suit up, he is also an upside WR2.\nJalen Coker\nNobody in this offense got there on Sunday, but Coker had his worst game of the season, catching 1 of 4 targets for 9 yards.\nIt was a total failure for the entire offense to give Coker some of a pass for the down week.\nHe has still been targeted on a team-high 25% of his routes this season, averaging 2.10 yards per route (WR19).\nCoker picked up a shoulder injury on Sunday, something to track in a short week.\nIf Coker is ready for Thursday, he is on the WR2/WR3 line but still has upside .\nCoker plays 45.9% of his snaps from the slot.\nIn the slot, he has been targeted on only 18.3% of his routes for 0.99 yards per route.\nGreen Bay has been excellent against slot receivers, allowing 5.1 yards per target (2nd) and a 2.2% touchdown rate (5th) to those receivers.\nWhile the slot matchup does Coker zero favors, he has been targeted on 30.6% of his routes for 3.04 yards per route out wide, where he can catch some of the same matchup notes highlighted earlier.\nSavion Williams\nWilliams caught all 3 of his targets for 47 yards on Sunday and added 2 rushes for 4 yards.\nWilliams is still more of a single-game DFS option, but he's taken control of the WR3 spot.\nWilliams ran a route on 67.4% of the dropbacks on Sunday, up from 59.1% in Week 6.\nPanthers vs. Packers Fantasy Tight End Preview\nTucker Kraft\nKraft secured 4 of 6 targets for 48 yards and a touchdown in Detroit.\nKraft got into the paint for the second time this season, which covered another week of lighter usage.\nHe had 14.3% of the targets.\nKraft has hit a 20% target share twice in a game this season.\nKraft is the TE16 in target share for the season (16.3%).\nOn a positive note, he ran a route on 80.4% of the dropbacks, his highest rate since Week 2.\nWith Golden coming into the week beat up, Kraft could be more active here as a back-end TE1 .\nThere is not a lot of matchup appeal here outside of touchdown production.\nCarolina ranks seventh in receptions allowed to tight ends (4.7 per game) and eighth in yards per target allowed to tight ends (5.9).\nThey have allowed a 59.6% catch rate to tight ends (3rd).\nHowever, they have allowed an 8.5% touchdown rate, which is 23rd.\nPanthers TEs\nBoth Tommy Tremble and Darren Waller have TE1 scoring weeks this season, but both of those games came with touchdowns.\nBoth are still touchdown-dependent TE2 options .\nWaller has been on the field for 53.3% of dropbacks, and Tremble for 41.2%.\nGreen Bay has also been really strong against tight ends, allowing a 66.7% catch rate (5th), 5.5 yards per target (4th), and a 4.8% touchdown rate (13th) to the position.\nMore Week 8 Fantasy Breakdowns From The Worksheet:\n[[[\"Week 8 NFL Games\",\"Time\"],[\" Panthers @ Packers \",\"Thursday Night Football\"],[\" Ravens @ Bills \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Vikings @ Lions \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Falcons @ Buccaneers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Colts @ Jaguars \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Titans @ Bengals \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Cardinals @ Cowboys \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Raiders @ Jets \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Browns @ Steelers \",\"Sunday -- 1 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chargers @ Rams \",\"Sunday -- 4:05 p.m. ET\"],[\" Chiefs @ Broncos \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Patriots @ Dolphins \",\"Sunday -- 4:25 p.m. ET\"],[\" Eagles @ Commanders \",\"Sunday Night Football\"],[\" Bears @ Seahawks \",\"Monday Night Football\"]],[{\"row\":0,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0},{\"row\":0,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":0,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":1,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcellstyle\":{\"font-weight\":\"bold\"}},{\"row\":1,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":1,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":2,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":2,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcellstyle\":{\"font-weight\":\"bold\"}},{\"row\":2,\"col\":1,\"visualRow\":2,\"visualCol\":1,\"prop\":1},{\"row\":3,\"col\":0,\"visualRow\":3,\"visualCol\":0,\"prop\":0,\"jtcel